// Studio workflows plus retained classic technical reference; no external help files needed.
struct HelpTopic {const char *title,*text;};
static const HelpTopic help_topics[]={
    {"Getting started",R"STUDIO_HELP(Imgtool Studio edits Midway IMG sprite libraries and their indexed palettes. The original imgtool remains a separate application.

1. Use Ctrl+O to open an IMG, or Explore sample to learn without touching game assets.
2. Browse sprites in the library using Up/Down or PageUp/PageDown. Select frames above the list makes row clicks check/uncheck batch marks; Done selecting keeps them. Ctrl-click toggles a mark, Shift-click marks a shown range. Tap Space to mark a sprite; Shift+M marks all and M clears marks. Marks choose batch inputs and do not edit the file. The Select pixels toolbar tool selects canvas pixels separately.
3. Choose a tool on the left; the color wheel edits the active RGB555 palette entry. Shared colors affect every sprite using that palette. Index zero remains transparent.
4. Use Tab to switch Artwork and Animation & alignment. Ctrl+Tab / Ctrl+Shift+Tab switches open IMG libraries. IMG animations edits stored sequences and scripts; Studio projects save separate .ISTUDIO compositions.
5. Ctrl+S saves; Ctrl+Shift+S chooses a destination. Save and Save As always retain the IMG's original version and record layout. Review LOAD2 packing before building game assets.

Each IMG and project keeps its own Undo history. Source editors have separate histories and Save controls. Closing or quitting protects applied unsaved edits. Escape cancels gestures and previews; File > Quit exits.

H or F1 opens this guide. Search checks topic titles and contents; Keyboard reference is generated from the actual command and tool bindings.)STUDIO_HELP"},
    {"Keyboard reference",R"STUDIO_HELP()STUDIO_HELP"},
    {"Select frames & batch marks",R"STUDIO_HELP(In Artwork, click Select frames above the left sprite list. Clicking a frame name or thumbnail now checks/unchecks its batch checkbox and previews it. Done selecting returns to ordinary browsing while keeping the checked frames. Ctrl-click toggles a mark in either mode. Shift-click checks the shown range from the last marking click; Shift-clicking a checkbox checks/unchecks that shown range. Filtering never changes hidden marks. Each library retains its own selection mode and marks.

Checkboxes, Space, Shift+M (mark all), M (clear all) and the Marks menu remain available. Checked frames feed batch geometry, palette operations, bulk PNG export and other commands with a Marked sprites scope. Marks are session state; they do not edit or save IMG flags, dirty the library or alter Undo/Redo. The toolbar's Select pixels tool (R) selects canvas pixels and does not mark frames. In Animation & alignment, the left list browses source artwork; actor frame transfers use their own review checkboxes.)STUDIO_HELP"},
    {"Selection & pixels",R"STUDIO_HELP(Each library keeps its own undo history. Shift adds to a selection; Alt subtracts; Shift+Alt intersects. Paint, erase and fill stay within selected pixels. Copy/cut preserve the shape, including selected transparency: tinted transparent pixels in the paste preview clear the destination. Copy without a selection leaves transparent pixels untouched when pasted. Nudging moves pixels without moving anchors; add Shift to move 10 pixels.)STUDIO_HELP"},
    {"Paint, line & fill",R"STUDIO_HELP(Line previews a round/square stroke: Shift snaps directions, release applies, Escape cancels. Replace keeps the color clicked at the stroke's start; All matching pixels replaces across the sprite or selection. Fill and Smart erase choose connected/global matching. Exact index is the default; Match palette colors includes duplicate slots and nearby shades at the chosen tolerance. Transparency stays separate from opaque black. Connected fills cannot cross selection holes.)STUDIO_HELP"},
    {"Clone stamp",R"STUDIO_HELP(Clone (C): Alt-click a source on the current sprite, then drag to copy pixels inside the selection. Each stroke freezes its source to prevent overlap feedback. Aligned strokes keep the offset; turn it off to start each stroke at the source. Copy transparent pixels explicitly enables erasing. Escape restores the unfinished stroke.)STUDIO_HELP"},
    {"Variant paint",R"STUDIO_HELP(Variant (V / T): choose an opaque palette color, drag to preview, or use Preview selected pixels. Recolors only the painted pixels in this palette while retaining their appearance under other palettes. Transparent pixels stay transparent. The review shows slot reuse, palette growth and LOAD2 PPP limits. Apply/Enter commits pixels and palettes in one undo step; Escape/Cancel/Undo discards the preview. Unused palette entries may be repurposed. No available slots blocks the complete edit.)STUDIO_HELP"},
    {"Blur & smudge",R"STUDIO_HELP(Blur (O) softens neighboring selected colors using one frozen source per stroke. Another stroke builds up the effect. Smudge (S) pulls colors along a drag, sampling the preceding brush position at pixel intervals. Both have round/square brushes and Strength controls, match results to existing opaque palette entries, preserve transparent outlines, and limit sampling and writes to the selection. Release commits one undo step; Escape restores the unfinished stroke and keeps its selection. Sparse palettes may limit the visible effect.)STUDIO_HELP"},
    {"Heal & defringe",R"STUDIO_HELP(Heal (J) replaces a brushed blemish by diffusing surrounding colors inward, using the frozen source and cumulative brushed area. Fill depth controls how far colors can travel. Defringe (F) cleans an outline band from the sprite interior; drag locally or Clean all/selected edges. Edge width is 1-4 pixels. Both use existing opaque palette colors, preserve transparency and keep unreachable repairs unchanged. Selection limits reads and writes. Release groups a stroke; Escape restores it and keeps the mask. Smart erase (E / U) has Clean erased edges enabled by default: surviving neighbors adopt safe local colors in the same undo step. Turn it off for a plain matching erase.)STUDIO_HELP"},
    {"Sprite overlay (L)",R"STUDIO_HELP(While placing a pixel paste or transforming clipboard pixels, press L or choose Edit > Keep paste as overlay. This keeps one separate indexed overlay on the current sprite. An existing overlay must be flattened or removed before adding another. Outside a paste, L selects Lasso.

The Sprite inspector controls visibility, X/Y position, one-pixel nudges (Shift = ten), horizontal/vertical flip, Flatten and Remove. Pasting as an overlay selects Move and the Overlay target automatically. With Move (Shift+V), choose Overlay or Base selection in the inspector. Drag a visible overlay inside its bounds; release applies one Undo step. Shift locks the dominant axis. Arrows move the overlay one pixel, Shift+arrows ten, without browsing frames. Escape or Undo cancels a drag without consuming history. Focus loss, workspace/sprite changes, modal dialogs and source edits cancel too. Space+drag still pans. Hidden overlays cannot be dragged; use coordinates to recover a completely off-canvas overlay. Base selection moves selected base pixels while the overlay stays in place, and both selection and line previews show the composite. Every overlay edit supports Undo/Redo. Negative positions and overhanging pixels are allowed; the canvas clips them. Duplicate sprite copies its overlay independently. Paint and pixel Copy operate on the base beneath it. Index zero always stays transparent on an overlay, including explicitly selected zeros that would clear pixels with ordinary Apply.

Save and Save As include the visible overlay as ordinary flattened pixels, keeping the original IMG version and layout. The live base and separate overlay remain editable until the library closes. Hidden overlays are excluded from output. Reopened IMG files contain flattened pixels and no editable layer. PNG, indexed LBM/TGA, TGA sheets and scene PNG export use visible appearance. LOAD2 checks use the flattened result. Geometry, region extraction, source palette remapping, compaction, variant color allocation and pipeline export require flattening or removing the relevant overlays first. Sprite copying between libraries also requires flattening the source overlay.)STUDIO_HELP"},

    {"Move & transform",R"STUDIO_HELP(Move (Shift+V) drags selected pixels into a floating preview. During Paste, Ctrl+T opens a transformed clipboard preview; H/V flips, arrows position, Enter or Ctrl+E applies, and Ctrl+J stamps while keeping another floating copy. Copy colors map to the destination palette; selection holes and explicitly selected transparent pixels remain distinct. Oversize clipboard transforms require increasing the canvas or Paste as New Sprite. Ctrl+T or Move / transform opens its inspector. Drag corners to resize, flip or rotate in 90-degree steps, or enable Duplicate instead of move. Enter or Apply commits one undo step; Escape, Cancel or Ctrl+Z discards the preview. Arrows move the preview by 1, Shift+arrows by 10. Keep it inside the sprite canvas. Anchors and metadata stay unchanged. Palette indices are retained with nearest-pixel sampling from the original selection. Changing the source or workspace cancels an unapplied preview.)STUDIO_HELP"},
    {"Blood palette lanes",R"STUDIO_HELP(Palette > Make room for blood, or the same button in Palette manager, condenses character colors to reserve a contiguous blood lane at the end of the selected palette. Default: 10 blood colors; adjust the count. For 64 slots, indices 1–53 hold character colors, 54–63 hold blood, and index 0 stays transparent. Total palette count and declared bit depth stay fixed, so this does not increase the LOAD2 palette-size fallback threshold.

New editable red ramp remaps existing artwork below the lane, then fills the freed slots with dark red through brighter highlights. New blank slots also frees them but fills them with opaque black; index zero alone is transparent. Keep existing blood tail protects those tail words and every pixel using them, condensing only the lower character colors. Choose Keep when the art already uses its tail as a blood lane.

Review every shared primary sprite in Before/After. The blood lane appears in its own swatch strip; expand All palette colors to inspect the full result. Colors pack exactly when they fit; otherwise a pixel-weighted merge reduces them. Allow approximate colors is required when shading changes; changing options resets approval. Apply blood lane is one Undo step. The blood swatches are selected afterward for editing with RGB controls or Shading / gradients. Then paint blood detail with these indices. This prepares palette colors; it does not automatically paint blood or assign game-engine blood behavior.

All sprites using this primary palette remap together. Alternate references or overlays block the operation until resolved because they share indices. Changed source data invalidates review. Sprite geometry, names, anchors, baselines, marks and other palette metadata remain intact. Save keeps the IMG's original version and record layout. A blood lane is ordinary indexed palette data; no new IMG fields or persistent slot locks are added. Other later palette operations can reuse or move these slots, so review them before applying.)STUDIO_HELP"},
    {"Bulk PNG export",R"STUDIO_HELP(Mark sprites in the library, then File > Export sprites as PNGs. Browse to an existing output folder or type its path and Build review. Marked sprites is the default when marks exist; otherwise Selected sprite. Shown by search and All sprites are also available. Uncheck rows without changing marks; inspect filenames, dimensions and transparency. Every included sprite becomes its own PNG at full size, using its palette and visible overlay. Index zero is transparent; nonzero black remains opaque. PNGs do not store IMG anchors or animation metadata.

Unsafe name characters become underscores; reserved filenames gain a prefix; duplicate names get __2, __3, etc. Review the exact names before exporting. Confirm Replace reviewed PNGs to overwrite existing files. Changed artwork or outputs require a new review. Up to 2000 sprites and 128 MiB of combined snapshots are supported. Outputs and backups are prepared before publication; ordinary replacement failures attempt rollback. Separate files are not globally atomic against interruption or concurrent changes. Source IMG bytes, original format, marks, selection and history remain intact.)STUDIO_HELP"},
    {"TGA sprite sheets",R"STUDIO_HELP(File > Export TGA sprite sheet reviews marked, shown, all or selected full sprites. Exclude rows without unmarking; inspect packed rectangles, anchors and face order, scroll to zoom and middle/right-drag to pan. Indexed output keeps matching palettes or combines exact used RGB555 colors; too many colors blocks instead of degrading art. RGBA supports per-sprite colors/alpha without ANF. Classic ANF stores anchors and faces in library order and requires a 256-wide indexed sheet, sprites up to 256x256, at most 1000 faces and 6000 rows. Both outputs are prepared first; second-replacement failure attempts to restore the first. Two files are not globally atomic against interruption or external changes. Build again after source or destination changes. Source IMG format, pixels, history and marks stay intact.)STUDIO_HELP"},
    {"TBL, IRW & ANILST",R"STUDIO_HELP(File > Export pipeline reviews TBL, IRW and ANILST output for marked, shown, all or selected whole sprites. Build after changing options or destination; exclude blocked rows before exporting. TBL SAGs and IRW pixels use the same uncompressed layout and depth settings, with Word/Nibble/Packed alignment and optional dual-bank addressing. TBL CTRL comes from bit depth, with optional MK3 secondary anchors and palette pointers. ANILST is a sequential sprite list. Source/destination changes require rebuilding; output replacement is atomic. These exports do not reproduce LOAD2 compression, deduplication, scaling or existing ROM allocation.)STUDIO_HELP"},
    {"LOD manifests",R"STUDIO_HELP(File > Open LOD libraries reviews LOAD2 manifests, paths and per-library PPP settings before opening separate IMG tabs. Rebuild after changing folders; resolve or exclude missing/invalid rows. Open tabs retain unsaved edits and current PPP. Other LOAD2 directives are listed but do not run: no ROM or table outputs are written. Cancel leaves libraries unchanged.)STUDIO_HELP"},
    {"Files & sprite commands",R"STUDIO_HELP(File > New IMG library (Ctrl+N), Import PNG sprite and Export selected PNG cover the basic file workflow. Sprite commands manage names (Ctrl+R / F2), copies (Ctrl+J), order and geometry. Right-click a library row for the same actions. The inspector assigns palettes or makes one private before recoloring. Structural edits and geometry changes undo with their metadata and selection.)STUDIO_HELP"},
    {"Animation & alignment",R"STUDIO_HELP(Animation & alignment (Tab): browse name-based frame groups, reconstruct subframes, scrub the timeline and press Space to play/pause. Arrows step frames. World View holds the anchor target fixed; drag a piece to edit its IMG anchor, or enable Drag the whole frame. Escape cancels the gesture. In Artwork, use Select to drag the yellow anchor handle.)STUDIO_HELP"},
    {"Studio projects & timing",R"STUDIO_HELP(Project > Create from current preview saves a composition as .ISTUDIO with its own undo history. Add actor lanes and cross-file frames, edit holds and positions, then save referenced IMGs before saving the project. Dragging an actor in a project edits scene placement, not IMG anchors. Motion & timing adds Show/Hide windows, velocity, maximum travel, a freeze tick and a second copy. Scheduled frames can overlap. Follow frame timing connects an effect to another actor's frame; retiming or reordering that frame moves the effect's start. Detach keeps the current start as an independent actor. This follows timing only, not position or visibility. Open project / import WAX accepts these row rules and uniform spawn runs. Manually moved or custom-timed spawn rows retain independent saved timing, matching classic; WAX import notes explains detached links. Remaining unsupported rules are reported. Export current scene PNG uses the same scene positions and timing as the canvas.)STUDIO_HELP"},
    {"Append project actors",R"STUDIO_HELP(Open or create a Studio composition, then Project > Append project actors. Choose another .ISTUDIO project or a supported classic .WAX. This adds its complete actor rows to the current composition instead of replacing it. Existing unsaved scene edits remain their own earlier Undo steps.

Review the incoming actor/frame/piece counts, reused IMG libraries, new tabs and scene settings before Append actors. The current playfield, origin, background, clock, mirror convention and view settings remain in use. Incoming actor coordinates and tick counts stay as authored; different origins or clocks can change where they appear or their speed in seconds. Imported actors retain multipart/cross-file pieces, holds, depth/facing, motion/caps, scale, opacity, freeze and second copies. Fresh actor/frame identities keep links between incoming effects together without binding them to existing frames that happened to have the same IDs.

Already-open IMG files are reused, including compatible unsaved edits. New libraries are staged until Apply; Cancel discards those staged tabs. Missing artwork uses Locate project artwork and leaves the active scene intact. Reviewed clean IMGs are checked again before Apply. All actors must fit the existing limits: 32 actors, 64 sources, 8192 frames and 65536 sprite references. The operation refuses an incomplete or over-limit append rather than skipping rows. WAX imports retain the same supported/guarded boundaries as ordinary WAX opening; import notes appear in the review.

One project Undo removes the imported actors and added references; Redo retains their identities. Newly opened source tabs stay open after Undo because Redo needs them. Save the current composition as .ISTUDIO. The incoming project and every IMG byte, format/version/record layout, artwork history and mark remain unchanged. No new native project version is introduced.)STUDIO_HELP"},
    {"Duplicate & order scene actors",R"STUDIO_HELP(In a Studio composition, select an actor and open Actions beside SCENE ACTOR in the inspector, or use the Project menu.

Duplicate actor copies all frames, multipart pieces, cross-library references and actor settings into an adjacent actor. It receives fresh actor/frame identities and starts independently at the original actor's current resolved start tick. Existing effects keep following the original actor. Drag the copy to reposition it. The original and copy can then be edited independently.

Bring actor forward and Send actor backward move the actor one step in draw order. At equal depth, the actor farther forward draws on top. Explicit frame depth takes priority over row order. Timing links, placement, selected frame and current timeline tick are preserved.

Each action is one project Undo step; Redo retains identities. Duplicate is limited to 32 actors, 8192 frames and 65536 sprite references; the resolved independent start must be at most 100000 ticks. Save as .ISTUDIO to retain the changes. Native project versions 1–6 stay unchanged. IMG bytes, original version/record layout, artwork history and marks are untouched.)STUDIO_HELP"},
    {"Copy / move scene frames",R"STUDIO_HELP(In a Studio composition, select an actor and choose Actions > Copy / move frames, Project > Copy / move frames, or right-click a frame thumbnail. Tick any frames from that actor, choose a destination actor and an insertion point, then Copy selected frames. Copy is the default; enable Move frames to transfer originals. Noncontiguous picks retain source-list order. The insertion point is a boundary in the original destination list; the same actor can be the destination.

Each frame keeps its hold, Show/Hide window, independent timing, offsets, depth, motion/caps, flips, second copy and multipart references across IMG libraries. Frames use the destination actor's placement, facing, opacity, scale, clock, freeze and loop settings, so appearance and start times can change. Copies get fresh identities and existing effects follow the originals. Moved frames keep their identities; effects following them retarget the destination actor. A move that would create a timing cycle is refused.

Leave at least one source frame when moving to another actor: Studio project versions 1-6 require nonempty actors. Limits remain 4096 frames per actor, 8192 frames and 65536 sprite references per project. Stale reviews and over-limit transfers leave the scene/history unchanged. One project Undo restores all frames and follower links; Redo retains identities. A same-actor move that changes nothing preserves Redo. Cancel changes nothing. Save as .ISTUDIO to keep the transfer. IMG bytes, original version/record layout, artwork histories and marks remain unchanged. This dialog selects frames from one actor; classic's arbitrary multi-row selection clipboard remains separate work.)STUDIO_HELP"},
    {"Reverse scene actor frames",R"STUDIO_HELP(Select an actor in a Studio composition, then Actions beside SCENE ACTOR > Reverse actor frames, or Project > Reverse actor frames. Every frame keeps its holds, multipart piece order, cross-file references, depth, facing and identity as the frame list reverses. Effects keep following the same frame at its new authored tick. The selected frame follows its identity and playback pauses. The timeline keeps its current tick, clamped if the scene becomes shorter.

As in classic's Reverse row command, timed Show/Hide windows mirror around the larger of the actor's total frame holds and its latest Hide tick. Frames with a window, explicit independent timing or motion are rebased to their old motion endpoint, then their velocity reverses. The endpoint is the exclusive Hide tick, or the total span for open-ended entries. A zero window value retains its default/open-ended meaning; explicit independent timing remains explicit.

Actor position, start/follow link, opacity, scale, freeze, looping, holds and motion caps are retained. This edits the row using classic's rules; it does not invert freeze/loop settings or recreate the delay after capped motion reaches its limit. Use Undo to restore the exact original instead of relying on reversing twice. Placements beyond +/-4096 or mirrored windows beyond 99999 ticks are refused with the whole scene unchanged. Each change uses one project Undo step; a true no-op preserves Redo. Save as .ISTUDIO. Native project versions 1–6 and IMG format/bytes/artwork history/marks remain unchanged.)STUDIO_HELP"},
    {"Split scene actor",R"STUDIO_HELP(Select a frame after the first in a Studio composition, then Split actor here in the timeline. The thumbnail right-click menu has Split actor at this frame, and Project has Split actor at selected frame. This ports classic's row-splitting workflow: earlier frames remain in the original actor; the selected frame and the rest move to an adjacent independent actor.

Both sequential lists start at the original actor's current start tick. Existing Show/Hide windows keep their actor-relative values. The new actor keeps multipart pieces in order, cross-file references, holds, offsets/depth, facing, motion/caps, freeze, second copies, magnification, visibility, opacity, loop and hold-end settings. A source that follows another actor keeps its own link; the new actor uses its current resolved start independently. Effects following a moved frame follow that frame in the new actor. Their start may move earlier when a sequential tail begins at frame zero.

One project Undo restores the original row and effect links; Redo retains identities. Failed splits leave the project/history unchanged. Both actors need a frame, the 32-actor limit applies, and an independent start must fit 0-100000 ticks. Save .ISTUDIO to retain the split; native versions 1-6 are unchanged. IMG pixels, palettes, anchors, stored sequence/script records, original file version/layout, marks and artwork history remain unchanged.)STUDIO_HELP"},
    {"WAX authored effect timing",R"STUDIO_HELP(Open project / import WAX now accepts spawned-effect rows whose first Show tick was moved by hand or whose Show/Hide windows were edited into a custom schedule. Classic drops their frame-following link when it sees these edits. Studio does the same and keeps their absolute Show/Hide times, motion/caps, freeze ticks, second copies, facing, magnification and holds. Later retiming of their original source frame does not move these independent rows. Other intact effects can still follow frames in an independent row.

Use WAX import notes beside the scene name to see which links detached and why. Reading or closing notes does not edit the composition or add Undo history. Save as .ISTUDIO to retain complete timing and references; successful saving clears the temporary import notes. The WAX file, referenced IMG bytes, original versions/layouts, marks and artwork history remain unchanged.

Uniform unedited runs keep frame-following. A missing spawn witness is allowed; the saved row hold determines uniformity even when its rate is not pinned. Incomplete windows, missing source rows/frames and cycles remain errors. An intact following row combined with a freeze tick remains guarded; an independently authored row retains its absolute freeze setting. Omitted generated composites, ASM-driven rows and embedded-script call previews remain separate work.)STUDIO_HELP"},
    {"Batch geometry",R"STUDIO_HELP(Batch geometry: mark sprites with the library checkboxes; Marks can mark every shown sprite or clear membership. Batch... or Sprite > Batch geometry opens a review for Marked, Shown by search, or All sprites in this library. Trim, resize by percent/exact size, canvas size and rotate/flip share the existing geometry behavior. Select a row and compare Before/After at one anchor target; sizes, anchors and cropped pixel counts are shown. Uncheck rows to exclude them. Finish editing settings to refresh the preview. Apply changes all enabled rows in one Undo step; Cancel/Escape leaves the IMG intact. Marks follow stable sprite identities through reorder/Undo and remain session view state. The batch edits whole sprites. Empty trims keep their original canvas, and geometry can affect LOAD2 packing.)STUDIO_HELP"},
    {"Restore child sprites",R"STUDIO_HELP(Sprite > Restore child sprites reviews parent-to-child pixel restoration in this library. Scope Marked, Shown by search or All children; choose a searchable parent or use a name rule with one capture (WALK1A -> WALK1). Build preview after settings change. Reconstruct keeps both silhouettes; Diff copies parent pixel changes since load and needs an equal-size baseline; Replace clears the child and copies overlap; Fill writes only transparent child pixels. Palette mismatches, missing/ambiguous parents and absent baselines are excluded. Partial coverage starts unchecked; Parent context shows child bounds in gold and uncovered areas in red. Before/After and Changes compare actual edits. Apply is one Undo step; Cancel/Escape discards the preview. Anchors, geometry and metadata stay intact. Pixel selections do not limit whole-child restoration. New or resized parents may lack a usable Diff baseline; choose another mode. Cross-library restoration is not part of this workflow.)STUDIO_HELP"},
    {"Timed subframe swap",R"STUDIO_HELP(In a Studio project, select a parent-only frame and choose Swap with subframes in the frame inspector. Set an actor tick (relative to that actor's start), or Use current tick. Review child names, inherited motion and before/after scenes, then Apply. All children draw together as one composite, not as a sequence of individual parts. At tick zero the handoff is immediate. A freeze before the requested tick must be removed or the tick moved earlier.

Matching uses classic's parent-name rules, the same source group and library order. POSE1A/POSE1B match POSE1; BASE_1 and BASE_2 match BASE. A plain BASE1/BASE2 set can match BASE too. Names and groups are case-sensitive, as in classic's command. Choose a frame with one parent sprite; multi-piece frames are already composites. The review requires 1–32 valid children and bounds captured source pixels/layers to 128 MiB.

The parent hides at the requested tick. One composite is inserted immediately after it, with all children at their existing anchors. Facing, depth, actor scale and remaining motion/caps are preserved without a jump. An exhausted cap stops its velocity instead of becoming unlimited. The composite starts with one copy; the parent's second copy ends with the parent. Parent hold follows classic's 1–120 tick clamp. Later sequential frames/effects may move when that hold changes; open-ended composites may coexist with later entries. Retiming an adjacent matching handoff replaces it with the same frame identity. Other adjacent composites stay unchanged.

Apply changes the project in one Undo; Redo restores the same frame ID. Cancel, stale/foreign/pending/tampered reviews and allocation failures preserve the composition/history/Redo. An identical handoff adds no history. Source IMG pixels, anchors, palette data, metadata and format remain unchanged. Save the project to keep the complete composite piece list. Native saves need no new version for ordinary positive-tick handoffs; explicit Independent timing at tick zero requires Studio project version 6. Versions 1–6 read; older versions stay in use when they represent the project.

Motion & timing has Independent timing to schedule an open-ended frame from actor tick zero. Nonzero Show/Hide values also schedule a frame. Clear resets Show/Hide and Independent timing, restoring sequential playback. Show here explicitly schedules even at tick zero.

Classic's WAX writer saves swap/waterline inputs and timing arrays but omits the complete generated entry-piece list. Importing those settings remains guarded; it cannot reliably recover a composite by guessing from a representative sprite. Open it in classic or rebuild the handoff in Studio. See Waterline cutting for individual fine-piece disappearance. This is composition preview/editing, not game script execution.)STUDIO_HELP"},
    {"Waterline cutting",R"STUDIO_HELP(In a Studio project, select a moving parent frame and choose Cut at waterline in the frame inspector. Enter world Y (positive down), or Use floor. Choose an open IMG and the parent whose named fine children will replace the moving frame. The fine source can differ from the parent artwork, and the moving parent may contain multiple pieces. Children use classic's case-sensitive parent-name/source-group rules and library order; 1–32 children are required.

The review finds the first full-canvas bounds crossing: bottom past the line when moving down, top past it when moving up. It uses actual Studio placement, vertical facing, both mirror conventions, actor magnification, motion, travel caps and freeze. The parent must have vertical motion and cross within 9999 actor ticks; no expiry is invented if it cannot reach the line. The minimum crossing interval is one tick. Unlike silhouette-based trimming, transparent canvas margins count.

The parent hides at the cut. Every fine piece starts there as an individual scheduled one-tick entry and hides separately when its own bounds cross. A capped or frozen piece unable to cross stays visible. Remaining motion/caps, facing and depth continue without a jump; the parent's second copy ends. Parent hold is retained. Inserting entries can shift later sequential frames/effects. Other frames stay in the project. A complete adjacent matching fine-piece run is replaced with its frame identities retained; incomplete or unrelated runs are retained.

Review per-piece hide ticks and scrub the original/proposed scene comparison. The red line is a review guide, absent from PNG export. Preview and export share the scene evaluator. Apply has one project Undo; Redo retains identities, and an identical review keeps Redo. Changed sources/project or failed reviews cannot partially apply. Save the Studio project for complete references/timing; source IMG pixels, palettes, anchors, metadata, version and record layout stay unchanged. Native versions 1–6 still read; this command needs no new file version.

Classic WAX files omit complete generated piece lists; old waterline settings remain guarded during import. Rebuild the cut in Studio or open it in classic. Studio computes crossings from its capped/scaled/frozen playback; classic's crossing estimate ignores those settings and may use a fallback lifetime.)STUDIO_HELP"},
    {"WAX embedded sequence recovery",R"STUDIO_HELP(Project > Open / import WAX accepts saved embedded sprite-sequence rows, including their old 10-source or newer saved special-slot boundary. The row's saved frame list is imported as a composition snapshot. Studio does not reload the current IMG record over that list or write scene edits back into it. Other recoverable saved rows are included alongside it.

The embedded source document/path, per-frame source overrides, holds (including long holds), local offsets, facing, magnification, depth, visibility, capped motion, freeze tick and second copies use the ordinary scene controls. Saved entry labels are retained; absent labels use source sprite names. Uniform effects following an embedded entry retain their timing connection. Missing files or sprite indices leave the open composition intact in Locate project artwork; Retry loading or Cancel there.

Save the result as .ISTUDIO for complete references and stable identities. The original WAX, source IMG records, file version and layout are unchanged. An explicitly empty row is not rebuilt from defaults or guessed from the IMG. Unsupported generated composites stay guarded. Embedded scripts remain separate: classic displays representative sequence-call thumbnails and pauses them; those are not ordinary playable sprite frames. Use Studio's IMG script inspector or open that WAX in classic.)STUDIO_HELP"},
    {"WAX dummy-body recovery",R"STUDIO_HELP(Project > Open / import WAX recovers generated dummy bodies when the file saves their source document/path and DECAP prefix. Studio resolves whole wrappers or ordered leg/torso pieces by name, using the same body-family rules as Build dummy body; it does not assume that a representative sprite contains the complete drawing. The saved source/prefix determines the body, including when classic last selected it automatically.

Old WAX files without slot.source_tabs use the legacy 10-row boundary; newer saved boundaries are honored. The reserved dummy slot becomes one ordinary Studio actor with the canonical 13-entry body order, complete piece references, saved facing, magnification, depth and per-entry settings. Inactive reserved ASM/embedded slots do not become extra rows. The stored 13 timing entries are retained; a pending body reset or incomplete timing array uses classic's stock timing and clears the same local motion/visibility fields. Whole-body data takes priority over parts, and ambiguous/incomplete families block loading. Uniform effects following a dummy entry retain their timing links.

All project sources bind together. Missing files, missing/ambiguous body families, invalid output or allocation failure leave the current composition unchanged. Locate project artwork reports the problem and permits Retry loading or Cancel. The imported composition is unsaved and uses ordinary actor controls/Undo; save it as .ISTUDIO to keep the recovered references. Reopening the native project keeps the full multipart list and stable identities. The WAX and source IMG files are untouched; IMG version/record layout never changes.

WAX files without a saved body selection, custom generated-body source overrides or spawned dummy rows still require rebuilding the body. Arbitrary omitted subframe composites, ASM-driven rows and embedded-script call previews remain separate migration work. This reconstructs the saved preview, not game-engine behavior.)STUDIO_HELP"},
    {"Dummy decapitation body",R"STUDIO_HELP(In a Studio project, choose Project > Build dummy body or Build dummy body in the scene inspector. Choose an open IMG and DECAP body family, review the seven source frames, stock timing and original/proposed scenes, then Add body actor. Scrub the actor-tick slider to inspect the motion. The initial source prefers a complete family in the current IMG, then another open IMG. When several families are available, whole-body coverage has priority. You always see and can change the source before applying.

Names match case-insensitively: LKDECAP1 through LKDECAP7 are whole frames. Alternatively every frame needs both LKDECAPLEG and LKDECAPTORSO pieces numbered 1–7; piece suffixes are allowed. Head sprites are excluded. Whole-body frames take priority where available; otherwise legs draw before torsos, with names sorted within each kind. Parts use their own original anchors. Duplicate whole-frame names are ambiguous and block the review. A source frame may contain at most 32 pieces; captured source pixels/layers share a 128 MiB review limit.

Classic's stock sequence is 1,2,3,4,3,4,3,4,3,4,5,6,7. Holds are 48,6,6,10,10,10,10,10,10,6,6,6,6: 13 entries totaling 144 actor ticks. A new actor defaults to facing opposite the selected actor, does not loop and holds its last frame. New actor faces left can change its facing before creation. The resulting actor is ordinary Studio content: edit its position, scale, holds, facing and timing through the existing controls. It saves complete references in .ISTUDIO and exports using the same scene evaluator.

Reset selected actor replaces its sequence with the chosen stock body. It clears frame-local offsets, motion/caps, visibility schedules and second copies. Actor name, placement, facing, opacity, scale, start/follow/freeze/loop/hold settings remain; surviving frame identities, frame facing and depth are kept by entry position. An effect following a frame that would be removed blocks reset until detached. Existing effects following retained entries stay linked and follow the new holds. Review the result, especially when retained actor visibility or freeze limits playback.

Apply is one project Undo. Redo restores the same identities; an identical reset preserves Redo. Stale, foreign, pending, tampered or failed reviews leave project/history intact. Cross-library sources remain held by the project and its history. Save referenced IMGs first, then the project. This command never changes IMG pixels, palettes, anchors, metadata, version or record layout, and needs no new Studio file version.

This is classic's canned body preview, not fatality game-script execution. WAX import can recover a generated dummy lane when the saved source and prefix resolve to a complete body family. See WAX dummy-body recovery.)STUDIO_HELP"},
    {"Actor magnification / WAX zoom",R"STUDIO_HELP(In a Studio project, Magnification in the actor inspector scales one actor from 1x to 4x. Each source pixel becomes a square block; source images, palette indices, primary/secondary anchors and IMG format remain unchanged. This is separate from zooming the whole canvas or resizing artwork.

Sprites grow about the actor origin: their anchor offsets, multipart spacing, frame offsets, second-copy offsets, motion and travel caps scale together. Actor placement and world origin stay in world pixels. Timing, visibility windows, depth, freeze ticks and Follow frame timing do not change. Dragging moves the actor in world pixels regardless of its magnification. Scale, drag, Undo/Redo and cancel affect the project only.

The canvas, hit testing, outlines, onion skins, facing ghosts, thumbnails and Export current scene PNG share the same scaled placement and nearest-pixel sampling, including both mirror conventions and vertical flips. The playfield clips magnified art. Magnification is a preview setting, not a claim about how the game scales an object.

Open project / import WAX reads each explicit row's zoom and clamps it to 1x–4x like classic; absent zoom means 1x. The original WAX remains unchanged. Save the imported composition as a separate .ISTUDIO file. Projects with any magnified actor use at least Studio project version 5 so older builds refuse them instead of silently showing 1x. Unscaled projects retain version 3 or version 4 for long holds; versions 1–6 read. Explicit independent timing uses version 6. These project versions never change an IMG's original version or record layout. Other unsupported WAX rules are still reported.)STUDIO_HELP"},
    {"Extract & split sprites",R"STUDIO_HELP(Sprite > Extract selection and Split body parts review child sprites before copying or cutting. Adjust part boxes and names, trim borders, and preview the remaining source. Children retain palette and primary-anchor alignment; existing animation references stay on the source. One Undo restores the entire extraction. For a new sprite on the original full canvas with both animation points inherited, use Split/Copy Selection to Overlay Frame instead.)STUDIO_HELP"},
    {"Split / Copy Selection to Overlay Frame",R"STUDIO_HELP(Select pixels, then choose Sprite > Split Selection to Overlay Frame or Copy Selection to Overlay Frame. The selection inspector has both actions. This creates a separate IMG sprite on the original full canvas, distinct from the pasted overlay layer. Split moves selected opaque pixels; Copy keeps the source unchanged. Selection holes and transparent pixels remain empty in the new frame. Pixels keep their original coordinates; there is no trimming or anchor rebasing.

The review shows Original source, Source after and New frame on identical canvases. Change Copy/Split or the frame name before applying. Names start with the first twelve source-name characters plus OVR; collisions receive _COPY, _COPY2, etc., ignoring case. Names accept up to fifteen printable characters without spaces. An empty name restores the default. Release the control or press Enter to refresh the preview.

The new frame inherits the palette, flags (including marked state), primary and all secondary animation-point words, alternate palette table and source group. Alternate tables are independent copies. As in classic, it starts a new record with frame number zero and no point table, baseline, pasted layer, raw name tail or source record offsets. Existing source metadata, point tables, baseline, pixel padding, palette colors and animation references remain unchanged. Source animation references do not automatically include the new frame.

One Undo restores the entire new-frame/source change; Redo restores the same sprite ID. The appended frame is selected and canvas selection is cleared. Cancel, no opaque selected pixels, stale/foreign/pending/tampered reviews and allocation failures preserve artwork/history/Redo. Pasted layers (including hidden/off-canvas) must be flattened or removed first. Unknown palette indices require repair. Commands are disabled in flipped, alignment or embedded-animation views. Limits: 8192 per dimension, 4 Mi-pixel source, 2000 total sprites and 128 MiB base-pixel library.

Save and Save As retain the original IMG version and layout. Adding a sprite or changing the source silhouette can change LOAD2 packing; review packing before building game data.)STUDIO_HELP"},
    {"Indexed LBM / TGA",R"STUDIO_HELP(File > Import indexed LBM / TGA reviews one full-canvas indexed sprite, keeps its source palette order or matches an existing palette, and lets you click the preview to place its anchor. LBM / IFF and indexed TGA drag-and-drop use the same review. File > Export selected LBM / TGA writes a separate indexed image without changing the IMG or history. LBM exports store the primary anchor as GRAB; TGA does not store IMG anchors. Settings and destination changes require rebuilding the review. Full-color TGA remains available through sprite-sheet import. File import also reviews sprite sheets and GIFs, digitizes source frames with material ramps, and captures video into the digitize review. Palette > Manage handles palette files, merging, compaction and gradients. File > Export pipeline writes reviewed TBL, IRW and ANILST files. Every existing IMG keeps its original version and record layout on Save / Save As. Edits that cannot fit that format cannot be saved. Pre-WIMP 5 and transitional 0x632 / 0x633 layouts, additional legacy image variants and game runtime remain on the migration list.)STUDIO_HELP"},
    {"Create scene from ASM",R"STUDIO_HELP(In Tools > ASM animations, load a frame table and resolve its artwork across open IMG libraries. Review artwork lets you choose among duplicate names. Create scene... opens a scrubbed composition review. Set Ticks/frame, facing, loop and object origin, then Build review after changing settings. The hold is an explicit preview choice; the frame table does not encode its caller/game animation speed.

Every multipart piece keeps its own IMG anchor. Frame offsets and horizontal/vertical flips follow the inspector. Create scene makes one unsaved actor in Animation & alignment with complete source references, stable IDs and normal project editing/Undo. Existing unsaved project changes offer Save/Discard/Cancel. Cancel keeps the old project; save the new composition as .ISTUDIO. IMG libraries remain referenced, so save unsaved sources before saving the project. Native reopening needs the IMGs, not the ASM source.

Partial source expansions, no-sleep frames, unresolved/ambiguous/stale symbols, invalid geometry or limits block creation. No-sleep timing cannot be represented as an ordinary held frame. Artwork/choices/source data are checked again at Create; if they changed, rebuild the review. Source pixels, palettes, anchors, history, files and IMG versions/layouts stay unchanged. Runtime routines, strike overlays, sound and game interactions are not transferred. This is an inspected-source-to-scene workflow; direct ASM-driven WAX recovery remains guarded because WAX omits resolved piece choices.)STUDIO_HELP"},
    {"ASM animations",R"STUDIO_HELP(Tools > ASM animations loads a read-only character ASM and resolves multipart frames across open IMG libraries. Choose a preferred library for duplicate names. Arrows step; Space plays; hold ticks override source timing. Flips, offsets, source segments and jumps are inspected; unsupported branches stop visibly and runtime calls are not executed. Feet to floor and object origin change preview placement only. Tools > MK2 strike tables edits an independent ASM document with its own Undo/Redo and reviewed Save. Animation source / Animate previews the move while you resize its strike box. Tools > Strike associations manages the classic .imgtool sidecar beside each strike source. Save associations remembers overrides, including None, with independent Undo/Redo and reviewed writes. Source switching retains unsaved sidecars; quit offers Save/Discard/Keep editing. Associations do not establish active-frame timing. IMG pixels, anchors, history and format stay unchanged.)STUDIO_HELP"},
    {"ILBM DEST plane mapping",R"STUDIO_HELP(Use File > Import indexed LBM / TGA or drop an .iff/.lbm file onto Studio. A DEST chunk can scatter stored source planes into different destination bit positions and fill the other bits with constants. Studio resolves complete mappings into ordinary indexed pixels before the import review. The review states how many source and destination planes are used; the original color-map order stays intact. Keep the source palette or match an existing palette, set the anchor, Build review after changes, then Import sprite for one Undo.

DEST needs eight bytes before BODY and an ILBM destination depth from 1 to 8. planePick must select exactly the stored source-plane count; successive source planes fill its set bits from low to high. Unpicked destination bits use planeOnOff. Bits above the declared destination depth and the reserved byte are ignored. A zero-plane source is accepted when DEST supplies every bit as a constant. Raw/ByteRun1 rows, masks and signed GRAB anchors use the existing path. Transparent-color keys are compared to source pixels before remapping; explicit mask coverage is retained. Opaque mapped color zero can move to a free slot under the normal transparency handling. Without a mask, destination index zero follows IMG transparency.

Every low destination bit must be writable in planeMask. A partial planeMask needs existing background bits; that input is rejected with a specific message instead of inventing a background. Merge it externally first. PBM DEST and malformed/depth-inconsistent mappings are rejected. Extra Halfbrite uses the effective destination depth and is supported when that depth is six, with its mode flag and base palette present. HAM stays unsupported.

Cancel and failed decoding leave artwork unchanged. Import/Undo/Redo and Save/Save As keep the original IMG version and record layout. LBM/TGA export stores resolved indexed colors and pixels, without recreating DEST metadata. The source IFF is never overwritten by import.)STUDIO_HELP"},
    {"Extra Halfbrite ILBM import",R"STUDIO_HELP(Use File > Import indexed LBM / TGA or drop an .iff/.lbm file onto Studio. Extra Halfbrite (EHB) requires ILBM with six destination planes, the Halfbrite CAMG mode flag, and at least 32 base palette entries. Studio resolves the base palette and its half-bright counterparts into 64 RGB555 colors while retaining the six-bit indices. Any stored upper palette entries are ignored in favor of derived colors; the review notes this explicitly. Palette channels are halved separately, without spilling bits between red, green and blue.

Raw and ByteRun1-compressed scanlines, explicit masks, transparent-color indices and signed GRAB anchors are supported. Without a source mask, index zero follows the existing IMG-transparency policy. With a mask, an opaque color zero can move to a free slot so its color stays visible. Review any remapping note. Keep source palette retains the resolved colors; choosing an existing palette uses the ordinary color-match review. Click the preview to place the anchor, Build review after changing settings, then Import sprite for one Undo. Cancel leaves the IMG intact.

Save/reopen keeps the destination IMG's original version and record layout. Exported LBM/TGA stores the resolved indexed colors; it does not recreate EHB display metadata. HAM, background-dependent destination merging and IFF ANIM remain unsupported. No display mode is guessed when its flag is absent. Malformed mode/depth/palette combinations are rejected.)STUDIO_HELP"},
    {"Copy / move libraries",R"STUDIO_HELP(Sprite > Copy / move to another library reviews sprites and required palettes between open libraries. Copy is the default. Enable Move from source to append them to the destination and remove them from the source. Both libraries are prepared before either changes. Each has its own Undo step; undo each separately to reverse the complete move, and Save both separately. Embedded-animation and open-scene references protect source sprites; Exclude referenced sprites keeps them. Keep at least one source sprite. Remaining native indices are remapped, source palettes are retained, and neither IMG format changes. External game references and closed projects are not scanned. Native animation records stay in their original library; use the animation transfer tools for records.)STUDIO_HELP"},
    {"Split IMG animations",R"STUDIO_HELP(File > Split IMG animations to new library creates a separate IMG tab with selected native sequences/scripts, called sequences and only the artwork/palettes they require. Animation > Split to new library offers the same review in IMG mode. Original names and the source IMG version/layout are retained; the source is unchanged. The new tab opens in IMG animations mode, has no creation Undo and participates in the unsaved-close guard. Save it when ready. Select an animation that references artwork; a library made only of empty records is not created. Unique source sprite names are required. Resolved damage dependencies and their global reference slots are included. This copies native IMG records, not scene projects or external game tables, and leaves every clipboard unchanged.)STUDIO_HELP"},
    {"Animation clipboard",R"STUDIO_HELP(File > Copy IMG animations to clipboard captures selected records, called sequences and their required artwork into an independent session snapshot. The source may close after copying. File > Paste IMG animations from clipboard reviews captured records against any open destination, with prefixes, manual sprite choices and Copy missing artwork enabled by default. Paste is one destination Undo step; Save separately. Animation menu offers the same commands in IMG animations mode. Edit > Clear animation clipboard releases it; Cancel or failed capture retains the previous clipboard. Pixel, palette and sprite clipboards are separate. Contents last until replaced, cleared or Studio closes. Capture retains names and requires unique source sprite names. Resolved damage dependencies are owned by the snapshot. Unresolved references, slot capacity and original-layout constraints still apply; IMG formats never change.)STUDIO_HELP"},
    {"Copy IMG animations",R"STUDIO_HELP(File > Copy IMG animations to library transfers sequences and scripts between open libraries. Selected scripts and damage bindings include required sequences/scripts transitively, including cycles. Enable Copy missing artwork to append referenced sprites that have unique missing names and their required palettes. Existing matches stay unchanged; ambiguous names still need manual choices. The sprite prefix also names new artwork. The review labels new sprites Copy and lists palette additions. Without that option, referenced artwork must already exist. Unique names match automatically; use Destination sprite prefix for prefixed artwork, or search each destination dropdown by name or index to bind differently named or duplicate sprites. Manual choices survive prefix and animation selection changes; changing libraries clears them. Reset sprite choices or choose Automatic name match to restore matching. Review sprite indices and geometry, and use an animation prefix for record name conflicts. Apply preserves stored timing, offsets and opaque bytes, remaps incoming targets, and opens the destination for inspection. One destination Undo step includes all copied artwork and records, no autosave and no IMG format changes. The review includes global damage-slot mappings (hover Damage bindings). Existing destination table bytes stay intact; identical remapped pairs share a slot. New references must fit signed-byte indices 0–127. Unresolved bindings, exhausted slots and incompatible older-layout fields are blocked; runtime script execution is not included.)STUDIO_HELP"},
    {"Sprite clipboard & split",R"STUDIO_HELP(Sprite > Copy to another library appends marked, shown or all sprites to a second open IMG. Review names, an optional prefix, palette assignments and exclusions. Exact palette reuse compares colors and metadata; otherwise required primary/alternate palettes are copied once. Copies keep pixel indices, anchors, points, baselines and metadata, with remapped alternate references. Name collisions, unsupported layouts and stale libraries block Apply. One undo removes destination additions; the source stays unchanged and both IMG formats stay fixed. This copies listed sprites without source sequences/scripts. Sprite > Split to new library creates a separate unsaved IMG tab with the source layout. Copy leaves the source unchanged; Move protects referenced sprites and removes eligible rows in one source undo step. Source Undo restores the sprites while the new tab remains. Save each library explicitly. Sprite > Copy sprites to clipboard captures an owned set that survives source edits and closure during this Studio session. Paste sprites from clipboard uses the same destination review and one undo step. Copy again to replace the snapshot, or clear it from Sprite/Edit. Pixel and palette-color clipboards remain separate. Include child pieces adds direct related sprites with parent labels; ambiguous matches are reported for explicit selection.)STUDIO_HELP"},
    {"Delete multiple sprites",R"STUDIO_HELP(Sprite > Delete multiple sprites reviews marked, shown or all sprites with a preview and exclusions. Embedded sequence and current open Studio scene references block deletion; Exclude referenced sprites keeps them automatically. Only listed rows are deleted and at least one sprite stays. Remaining embedded sprite indices are remapped while script sequence indices remain intact. One undo restores pixels, metadata, references and selection. IMG version and layout stay fixed. Child pieces are not added implicitly; external files and closed scenes are not scanned.)STUDIO_HELP"},
    {"Palette assignment & cleanup",R"STUDIO_HELP(Palette > Remove unused palettes reviews all primary and alternate uses, with per-palette keep choices and one undo step. Retained references are remapped, without changing colors, pixels or the IMG format. External palette-name references are not scanned.

Palette > Merge duplicate palettes reviews exact matches across the library. Color count, declared depth and every stored word must match, including zero/high bits. The first match retains its name/metadata; later duplicates start checked. Uncheck individual rows or Keep all to preserve separately named palettes. Only duplicates filters the list; selecting a row shows the first match, original/resulting indices and colors. Unique palettes and palettes with different colors/depths stay separate. Apply remaps primary and active alternate references without changing pixels, appearance, layers, baselines or embedded data. Default alternate indices and bytes at/after the terminator are preserved. One Undo reverses the whole merge; Cancel changes nothing. Stale reviews cannot apply, and allocation failure leaves library/history unchanged. Save separately in the existing IMG version/layout. ASM/TBL and closed-project palette names are not scanned; keep identities required by those files.

Palette > Assign to multiple sprites reviews marked, shown or all sprites with a before/after primary-palette preview. Match colors keeps transparency and maps opaque colors to opaque destination slots; approximate colors require review. Keep indices changes the palette reference without changing pixel bytes. Remaps that would alter active alternate-palette appearances are blocked; keep indices or exclude those sprites. Apply uses one undo step and keeps metadata, baselines and the IMG version/layout. Palettes are not resized or modified; check LOAD2 packing after palette-size changes.)STUDIO_HELP"},
    {"Batch rename",R"STUDIO_HELP(Sprite > Batch rename previews numbered names, prepended text or appended text plus numbers for marked, shown or all sprites. Names are limited to 15 printable ASCII characters without spaces. Excluded rows retain their current names and other preview numbers do not move. Collisions against the whole library block Apply. All changed names share one undo step; pixels, metadata and embedded references remain intact. IMG version and layout stay fixed. External ASM / TBL names are not rewritten, and naming changes can affect LOAD2 allocations.)STUDIO_HELP"},
    {"Split with IMG animations",R"STUDIO_HELP(Sprite > Split to new library offers Include IMG animations with Copy. It copies sequences using only included sprites and scripts whose called sequences are copied. Damage-bound records require their complete dependency chain within the reviewed artwork set; their global references are remapped. Review copied/skipped records: empty records without dependencies, outside or unresolved references and records depending on skipped animations stay out. The sprite set never expands automatically; use Include child pieces or change exclusions to cover a complete animation. The source stays unchanged; the new IMG inherits its original layout and starts unsaved. Move from source is separate and cannot include animation records.)STUDIO_HELP"},
    {"Fatality source workspace",R"STUDIO_HELP(Tools > Fatality source keeps up to 16 ASM sources open: commands, scom_* combos, or animation data. Add source retains other files and their histories. The source selector restores the last block in each file. Reload source and Close source protect applied edits; Quit checks every modified source. Linked combos prefer the current file, then unique matches in other open sources; choose explicitly if several match. Save As cannot overwrite another open source. Each file saves separately. The label line stays fixed; edit indented body lines, Apply block, then review Save source. Source Undo/Redo is separate from IMG history. Switching blocks resets unapplied text. In-place saves reject external changes, and close/reload/quit protect applied unsaved edits. Inspect animation snapshots applied edits in all open sources, follows unique external jumps and frame groups, and reports ambiguous symbols. Review artwork lists every expanded symbol, counts its uses, locates its first frame, and offers explicit choices among matching sprites in open IMG libraries. Choices are shared with the canvas and reaction table; they last for the loaded source and reset on successful ASM load. Refreshing the artwork review keeps choices. Closed or renamed choices stay visibly unavailable until restored or chosen again. Frame locations identify their defining files. Back to fatality source returns to editing; inspect again to refresh the snapshot. This does not assemble code, follow includes, run game routines or infer attacker/victim timing. Existing IMG formats and artwork stay unchanged.)STUDIO_HELP"},
    {"Reactions",R"STUDIO_HELP(Tools > Reactions groups the loaded character ASM by Hit, Stagger, Knockdown, Throws, Getup and Death. Search includes thrower names and classification reasons; Show other animations exposes entries the suggestions missed. Choose a frame/piece row to scrub, then Open selected sprite to edit its IMG anchor. Frame anchors refresh after edits, Undo, library changes and facing/convention changes. Missing or ambiguous pieces stay explicit. Placement is relative to object origin using each library's mirror convention. Source ASM is read-only; game routines and attack/reaction timing are not executed or inferred.)STUDIO_HELP"},
    {"Strike phase inspector",R"STUDIO_HELP(Tools > Strike phase inspector shows which box the loaded source selects as a manually supplied active countdown decreases. Assume stkphase / wunit flags affect only this preview, including conditional slot numbering; build files stay unchanged. Red is the selected box, gray is its base reference. Edit selected strike opens the existing ASM editor. Edit phase rows stages existing thresholds and slots, previews the selection, and applies through the source undo history. Review source save opens the ASM diff before writing. Row counts stay fixed, thresholds decrease to zero, and all consumers must resolve under the chosen preview flags. Build settings and IMG format stay unchanged. Artwork frames are chosen independently; startup, recovery and hit interruption are not inferred.)STUDIO_HELP"},
    {"Frame origins & animation points",R"STUDIO_HELP(The frame origin is local pixel coordinate (0,0), the top-left of the rectangular buffer. Transparent padding is part of that rectangle. X increases right; Y increases down.

Primary animation point (AX/AY) is a signed offset from that origin and can lie outside the rectangle. At world anchor (WX,WY):
    sprite top-left = (WX - AX, WY - AY)
    pixel (x,y) = (WX + x - AX, WY + y - AY)

Choose the same physical feature in each frame to keep animation stable. In Artwork use Select (R) to drag the yellow anchor handle, edit inspector values, or use Animation & alignment to drag pieces around a fixed world anchor. Link animation anchors offers explicit reference and target points. Onion skin and facing preview help compare poses.

Secondary AX2/AY2/AZ2 is another point inside the same frame, not a link to another frame. Stored FFFF/FFFF/FFFF means unused; zero is a valid active point.

IMG animations stores sequence entries (sprite index, ticks, offsets and spare words) and script calls. Reordering the name-based preview does not write a stored animation. A Studio project edits scene placement separately from IMG anchors.

Horizontal mirroring uses the library's selected convention; engine paths may differ by one pixel. Preview both facings and verify the convention used by the game's routine.)STUDIO_HELP"},
    {"IMG, palettes & file formats",R"STUDIO_HELP(IMG is the primary binary little-endian sprite-library format. Each file includes a library header, image/palette records, pixel and palette blocks, and optional embedded sequences, scripts, point tables, alternate palettes and version-specific fields.

Modern IMAGE records are 50 bytes with a 16-byte name. PALETTE records are 26 bytes with a 10-byte name. Earlier layouts differ: Studio retains the loaded version and layout on both Save and Save As. Unsupported layouts are rejected, never converted.

In-memory indexed pixels use a four-byte-aligned row stride: (width + 3) & ~3. Palette colors are RGB555: five bits per red, green and blue channel. Index zero is transparent. Default palettes #0/#1/#2 are implicit on disk; stored palettes begin at #3. Names can contain preserved bytes after the null terminator, which LOAD2 may hash for allocation. Renaming can affect the resulting packing.

TGA: indexed color-mapped exchange, or reviewed RGBA sprite sheets. Classic ANF face metadata has stricter sheet size and frame-count constraints; see TGA sprite sheets.
LBM / IFF: indexed ILBM/PBM variants supported by the importer, with CMAP and BODY chunks and supported RLE compression. Unsupported variants report an error.
PNG: RGBA import/export, quantized to Midway indexed colors on import. Color zero exports with transparency.
GIF / video: frame capture and digitize/import reviews produce indexed sprites and optional IMG sequences. Video needs an ffmpeg executable.
TBL / IRW / ANILST: external game-pipeline exports, not replacement IMG formats. See export topics for packing limits.
ISTUDIO: a separate composition/project format with references to IMG assets. Save referenced IMGs separately.

Studio applies its own bounded import and allocation limits, with errors for unsupported geometry or layouts. The old help's 640x400 limit and automatic pre-2.x conversion instructions do not apply.)STUDIO_HELP"},
    {"DMA2 hardware reference",R"STUDIO_HELP(
        THE BRAND SPANKING NEW DMA (#2)

            KEEP ENTERPRISES, INC.

            JANUARY 1, 1992

            DOCUMENT REV. 1.5


    DMA # 2 - GENERAL INFORMATION

    - THE NEW DMA WILL INCORPORATE BACKWARD COMPATIBILITY TO THE OLD DMA
    IN BOTH PINOUT AND FUNCTIONALITY.

    - THE NEW FEATURES IN ADDITION TO THE OLD ARE AS FOLLOWS:

        1) VARIABLE PIXEL SIZE PROCESSING.  THE NEW DMA CAN PROCESS
           1 TO 8 BIT PIXELS THAT ARE STORED IN IMAGE MEMORY IN A
           SERIAL FASHION.

           EXAMPLE:  5 BIT PIXELS STORED INTO 8 BIT EPROM
           +---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+
           |P1 |P1 |P1 |P1 |P1 |P2 |P2 |P2 |
           +---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+
           |P2 |P2 |P3 |P3 |P3 |P3 |P3 |P4 |
           +---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+
           |P4 |P4 |P4 |P4 |P5 |P5 |P5 |P5 |
           +---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+
           |P5 |P6 |P6 |P6 |P6 |P6 |P7 |P7 |
           +---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+

        2) THE NEW DMA CAN BE HALTED IN THE MIDDLE OF A TRANSFER
           AND THEN BE RESTARTED TO RESUME THE TRANSFER.  THIS IS
           ACCOMPLISHED BY WRITING A ZERO TO THE DMA GO BIT (BIT 15)
           IN THE CONTROL REGISTER.  IN THE OLD DMA, THIS WOULD KILL
           THE TRANSFER SO THAT IT COULD NOT BE RESTARTED.  TO KILL
           A TRANSFER IN THE NEW DMA, WRITE A ZERO TO THE DMA GO BIT 2
           TIMES IN A ROW.  TO RESTART A TRANSFER AFTER HALTING,
           WRITE A ONE TO THE DMA GO BIT IN THE CONTROL REGISTER.

        3) IN ADDITION TO THE CLIPPING ACHIEVED BY MANIPULATING THE
           OFFSET REGISTER, IN THE NEW DMA, A METHOD OF CLIPPING
           USING REGISTERED CLIP VALUES IS AVAILABLE. THE HOST CAN
           SPECIFY CLIP AMOUNTS TO THE DMA AND THE MATH NEEDED TO
           IMPLEMENT A TRANSFER IS DONE INTERNAL TO THE DMA.

        4) THE NEW DMA CAN DO A TRANSFER FROM THE IMAGE MEMORY TO THE
           BIT MAP WITH A SCALING EFFECT, I.E. THE IMAGE CAN BE
           SHRUNK OR ENLARGED.

        5) THE NEW DMA IMPLEMENTS A COMPRESSION MODE IN WHICH LEADING
           AND TRAILING ZERO DATA PIXELS CAN BE ENCODED IN A RUN LENGTH
           FASHION TO SAVE ON IMAGE MEMORY.

        6) OFF SCREEN CLIPPING CAN BE AUTOMATIC.  THERE ARE FOUR
           REGISTERS THAT SPECIFY THE WINDOW TO WHICH THE DMA CAN
           TRANSFER DATA.

        7) NOTE THAT THE CONTROL REGISTER AND THE OFFSET REGISTER
           HAVE BEEN SWAPPED SO THAT THE MOVE MULTIPLE INSTRUCTION
           CAN BE USED TO DOWNLOAD THE REGISTERS AND SET DMA GO
           EFFICIENTLY.

    DMA # 2 - INTERNAL REGISTERS R5-R0

        REGISTER # 7 - SOURCE VERTICAL SIZE REGISTER
        +---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+
        | 0 | 0 | 0 | 0 | 0 | 0 |           VERTICAL SIZE              |
        +---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+

        REGISTER # 6 - SOURCE HORIZONTAL SIZE REGISTER
        +---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+
        | 0 | 0 | 0 | 0 | 0 | 0 |          HORIZONTAL SIZE             |
        +---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+

        REGISTER # 5 - DESTINATION ADDRESS - Y
        +---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+
        | 0 | 0 | 0 | 0 | 0 | 0 | 0 |      DESTINATION Y COORDINATE     |
        +---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+

        REGISTER # 4 - DESTINATION ADDRESS - X
        +---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+
        | 0 | 0 | 0 | 0 | 0 | 0 | 0 |      DESTINATION X COORDINATE     |
        +---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+

        REGISTER # 3 - SOURCE ADDRESS - HIGH ORDER
        +---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+
        |               SOURCE ADDRESS UPPER 16 BITS                   |
        +---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+

        REGISTER # 2 - SOURCE ADDRESS - LOW ORDER
        +---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+
        |               SOURCE ADDRESS LOWER 16 BITS                   |
        +---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+

        REGISTER # 1 - CONTROL REGISTER   ** SEE NOTE 1 BELOW
        +---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+
        |DGO|  PIX SIZE |TM1|TM0|LM1|LM0|CMP|CLP|VFL|HFL|   PIXEL OPS   |
        +---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+

        REGISTER # 0 - OFFSET REGISTER / RCLIP-LCLIP VALUES
        +---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+
        |            OFFSET VALUE FOR OLD STYLE CLIPPING               |
        |   LEFT CLIP PIXELS VALUE     |    RIGHT CLIP PIXELS VALUE    |
        +---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+

        ** NOTE 1:
        DGO      - BIT 15     - DMA GO / DMA HALT
        PIX      - BITS 14-12 - PIXEL SIZE (0 = 8 BITS)
        TM1      - BIT 11     - DMA COMPRESS TRAIL PIX MULT BIT 1
        TM2      - BIT 10     - DMA COMPRESS TRAIL PIX MULT BIT 0
        LM1      - BIT 9      - DMA COMPRESS LEAD  PIX MULT BIT 1
        LM0      - BIT 8      - DMA COMPRESS LEAD  PIX MULT BIT 0
        CMP      - BIT 7      - DMA COMPRESS MODE
        CLP      - BIT 6      - DMA CLIP ON = 1 (USING U,D,L,R METHOD)
        VFL      - BIT 5      - VERTICAL FLIP (FLIP ABOUT X AXIS)
        HFL      - BIT 4      - HORIZONTAL FLIP (FLIP ABOUT Y AXIS)
        OPS      - BITS 3-0   - PIXEL CONSTANT/SUBSTITUTION OPS

        ** NOTE 2: IN COMPRESSION MODE, SCALING IS INHIBITED AND
                   CLIPPING IS INHIBITED.


    DMA # 2 - CLIPPING AN IMAGE

    OVERVIEW: AN IMAGE CAN BE TRANSFERRED TO THE BIT MAP IN ITS
    ENTIRETY OR A PORTION CAN BE "CLIPPED" BY SPECIFYING LEFT AND
    RIGHT CLIP AMOUNTS IN REGISTER 0, WITH BIT 6 (CLP) SET.

    IMPLEMENTATION:
            - THE OFFSET METHOD (OLD STYLE): REGISTER 0 UPPER BYTE =
              LEFT CLIP, LOWER BYTE = RIGHT CLIP.
            - THE REGISTER CLIP METHOD (NEW FEATURE): SET CLP BIT TO 1,
              USE REGISTERS 12 AND 13 FOR WINDOW BORDERS.


    DMA # 2 - TRANSFERRING A SCALED IMAGE

    OVERVIEW: AN IMAGE CAN BE SCALED BY TRAVERSING EACH LINE WITH A
    PREDETERMINED SAMPLE RATE.  RATIO IS 1:(INT + FRAC/256).
    REGISTER 11 = Y SCALE, REGISTER 10 = X SCALE.
    SCALE FACTORS: UPPER BYTE = INTEGER, LOWER BYTE = FRACTION.

    MAXIMUM SCALE FACTOR FOR SHRINK IN X DIRECTION:
    #BITS/PIXEL    INT  FRACTION
    -----------    ---- --------
         1          1F    FF
         2          10    00
         3          0A    AA
         4          08    00
         5          06    66
         6          05    55
         7          04    92
         8          04    00


    DMA # 2 - COMPRESSION OF LEADING AND TRAILING ZEROS

    TO SAVE IMAGE SPACE, LEADING AND TRAILING ZERO PIXELS ARE RUN-
    LENGTH ENCODED. THE FIRST BYTE OF EACH COMPRESSED LINE CONTAINS:
    UPPER NIBBLE = TRAILING ZERO COUNT, LOWER NIBBLE = LEADING ZERO
    COUNT. TMx/LMx BITS IN CONTROL REGISTER MULTIPLY THESE VALUES BY
    1, 2, 4, OR 8.

    IN COMPRESSION MODE, THE DMA DECODES THIS ON THE FLY IF:
      CMP (BIT 7) = 1, TMx, LMx BITS = 0.


    DMA # 2 - OFF SCREEN CLIPPING (WINDOWING)

    FOUR REGISTERS SPECIFY WINDOW BOUNDARIES (0-511).  SET CONFIG
    REGISTER BIT 5 = 0 FOR LEFT/RIGHT, = 1 FOR UPPER/LOWER.
    REGISTERS 12/13 HOLD LEFT/TOP AND RIGHT/BOTTOM LIMITS.


    DMA # 2 - LIMITATIONS

    SCALING: THERE ARE SIZE LIMITS. SEE TABLE ABOVE FOR SHRINK/GROW
    MAX/MIN IN X DIRECTION. 1-BIT PIXELS CAN SHRINK TO 1F.FF (1:31.996).
    COMPRESSION: CLIPPING AND SCALING ARE DISABLED IN COMPRESS MODE.
)STUDIO_HELP"},
    {"Classic shortcuts & migration",R"STUDIO_HELP(Original imgtool shortcuts are retained where Studio has the corresponding operation. Review dialogs replace immediate destructive actions:

Ctrl+Shift+C / X opens extraction review with Copy / Cut selected; Ctrl+Shift+V creates a new sprite from the pixel clipboard in one Undo step.
Ctrl+P opens point-table review, including enable/remove, instead of immediately toggling the stored block.
[ / ] changes brush size with a brush tool, otherwise opens marked / selected palette assignment review. Choose the destination palette in that review. Shift+R opens palette Rename. * opens palette Merge review. Palette manager owns Ctrl+C/V, Delete, Up/Down and Undo/Redo while its dialog is active. It does not change the IMG behind another modal.
; opens a marked-sprite 50% smooth reduction review; review size and quality before Apply. Ctrl+B opens marked TGA sheet export. Alt+L and Ctrl+L open indexed import; Alt+S exports selected LBM.

H opens Help; Shift+H selects Pan. V selects Variant Paint (T is also available); Shift+V selects Move. E selects Smart erase (U also works); Shift+E selects plain Erase. K plays/pauses; Shift+K selects Line. Tooltips and Keyboard reference show these bindings.

Tab switches Artwork / alignment. Ctrl+Tab switches libraries. Tap Space to mark in Artwork; hold Space and left-drag to pan without marking or painting. Space plays in animation workspaces. Studio supports explicit review before deleting sprites with Shift+Delete; Delete/Backspace clear a selected pixel region. Without a selection, Delete opens sprite deletion review. Escape cancels a gesture rather than quitting the application.

The classic floating-paste L command keeps an editable sprite overlay; see Sprite overlay (L) for session and save behavior. Ctrl+Left/Right reorders the temporary name-based preview, stored IMG entries in IMG animations, or project frames in a project. Only the latter two affect their own document histories. In Palette manager, Space marks the selected palette, Shift+M marks all and M clears marks. Select a destination and press * (or keypad multiply) to review all marked sources together.)STUDIO_HELP"},
    {"Remove palette slots",R"STUDIO_HELP(Palette manager > Remove slots and the swatch right-click action Remove selected color slots open a palette-wide review. Selected opaque swatches are targets; with no opaque selection, the clicked/highlighted nonzero slot is the fallback. Toggle targets in review; index zero stays protected. This operation deletes slots from the shared palette. Pixels in primary users of removed slots become transparent, while surviving colors keep their stored words/order and their pixel indices shift down. All primary and active alternate users are reviewed; marked sprites and canvas selections do not restrict it. Individual users cannot be excluded from a shared palette change. Use Make colors transparent when you want to keep the slots or limit the scope instead.

The slot map shows each old index, its new index, or transparent/deleted. Before/after previews show this selected palette's appearance, including visible overlays; alternate-only users are labeled. The result reports removed slots, cleared primary pixels, shifted primary indices and blocked users. Resolve every blocker before Apply: overlays whose referenced indices would change need flattening/removal, and active alternate appearances whose shared indices would change need reference resolution. Unused-tail removals are allowed when all relevant base/layer indices remain fixed. Unresolved source indices are refused. An unused palette can remove slots without sprites; removing all opaque slots leaves index zero only.

Declared palette depth is preserved, as in the original tool. PPP is a review-only color-limit comparison; zero disables it. Removing slots may cross the LOAD2 palette-size limit; removing pixels changes silhouettes and can affect zero-shape packing. Verify LOAD2 packing before building game data. Names, other metadata, assignments, sprite IDs, baselines, point tables, alternate tables, padding and independent clipboards remain unchanged. The canvas selection remains after Apply; palette swatch selection clears because slot numbers changed, and the highlighted slot follows the map. Apply is one Remove palette color slots Undo step. Cancel changes nothing. Changed palettes/users/pixels/overlays invalidate review, and allocation failure preserves artwork/history/Redo. Save keeps the existing IMG version and record layout.)STUDIO_HELP"},
    {"Palette transparency",R"STUDIO_HELP(Make palette colors transparent: in Palette manager, select color swatches and choose Make colors transparent, or right-click a swatch for that action. If no opaque colors are selected, the clicked/highlighted nonzero slot is the fallback. The review can toggle colors and choose Current sprite, Canvas selection only, Marked on this palette, or All sprites on this palette. Current/selection refer to the selected artwork sprite when the review opened, not the palette preview selector. Scope is fixed to the chosen source palette; marked users on other palettes and alternate-only references stay outside it. Arbitrary canvas selection masks keep their holes. A missing selection or empty color/sprite scope disables Apply. Index zero is protected.

The before/after base-pixel preview and per-sprite rows show pixels that become index zero. Uncheck rows to exclude them. Overlays and active alternate appearances that would lose pixels require resolution or exclusion; no-matching-pixel alternate users remain unchanged. Unknown source indices need repair or exclusion. Palette words, slots, count/depth, assignments, sprite identity, baselines, point tables and pixel padding stay fixed. This does not delete palette slots or move higher indices. Apply records one Make palette colors transparent Undo step; Cancel changes nothing. No matching/included edits add no history. Changed sources invalidate reviews; allocation failures preserve artwork/history/Redo. Pixel, palette and swatch clipboards stay independent. Save retains the existing IMG version/layout. Removing pixels changes silhouettes and may change LOAD2 zero-shape packing; verify packing before building game data.)STUDIO_HELP"},
    {"Sprite Ramp Gradient",R"STUDIO_HELP(Sprite > Sprite Ramp Gradient, or Sprite inspector > More, sweeps the sprite's tonal shading across a new contiguous palette-index ramp. This can give low-bit-depth art more shading steps. Palette gradients recolor existing slots; opacity gradients remove pixels. Sprite Ramp Gradient writes new opaque indices while keeping transparency, silhouette, geometry, anchors and IMG format fixed.

Choose Current sprite, Canvas selection or Marked sprites; individual rows can be excluded. Canvas selection limits writes, including mask holes; bounds, edge distances and tonal shading still come from the whole original sprite. The review captures every primary/active-alternate user of every target palette, including hidden/off-canvas overlays. All live indices stay reserved, including the target's own original indices, so band-limited sweeps retain their old colors beyond the band. All included palettes receive the same lowest contiguous free block. Excluding a palette's last eligible target recomputes placement and leaves that palette unchanged.

Colors has the ramp index ceiling (1–8 bpp), requested size, Fill free run and 2–11 editable RGB stops. From sprite seeds stops from the original tones; one tone seeds black to that color. Reverse flips stop order. Requested size can exceed the available shared block: the actual placed count and effective shading width are shown. No free block of at least two opaque indices blocks Apply. The ceiling limits new ramp indices; it does not lower surviving higher source indices, existing palette size or declared depth. Actual pixel depth is reported separately. Only unused ramp slots are repainted; other stored words stay exact. Palette count/depth grow as needed and never shrink.

Sweep offers four axes, two radial directions and silhouette-following Edge inward, content/canvas bounds, a limited pixel band, endpoints, shading width, and None/Noise/Checker/Bayer dithering. Shading width 1 washes the silhouette; full-ramp shading width re-shades by original luminance with no spatial travel. Intermediate widths move light/dark tones together across the ramp. Pixels beyond the band keep their old indices. Noise uses coordinates/source color/library index and a seed within +/-1000000. Release controls or press Enter to update the planned result. The tested classic sprite_gradient math is shared unchanged.

Saved shares Studio's palette-gradient presets. Choosing one changes the review. Save preset writes settings immediately, replacing the same name; it does not edit IMG/history. Presets store RGB8 stops, so reloading can quantize float colors. Malformed saved data is preserved and cannot be overwritten through this review. Classic settings are separate.

Review before/after base pixels, the quantized ramp bar, index/RGB changes and each palette's count/depth/PPP impact. Changed rows with any overlay or active alternate references need resolution or exclusion. No included opaque edits add no history or discard Redo. Apply recaptures shared users, padded pixels, raw palette fields/words, alternate tails and layers; recomputes usage/output; and records one Sprite ramp gradient Undo step for every included palette and sprite. Stale/foreign/pending/tampered reviews or allocation failures cannot publish partial ramps. Captures are bounded to 2000 targets, 8192 per dimension, 4 Mi-pixel sprites, 64 Mi-pixel shared-user work and a 256 MiB pixel/scratch budget.

New indices and palette growth can affect LOAD2 packing, including unchanged sprites sharing those palettes. PPP is review-only. Verify packing before building game data. Save and Save As retain the original IMG version and record layout.)STUDIO_HELP"},
    {"Sprite opacity gradient",R"STUDIO_HELP(Sprite > Opacity gradient, or Sprite inspector > More > Opacity gradient, reviews a spatial fade on Current sprite, Canvas selection or Marked sprites. IMG stores indexed pixels, not per-pixel alpha: opacity is simulated by clearing selected opaque pixels to transparent index zero. Surviving indices, palette words/count/depth and assignments stay fixed. This is a pixel operation; Palette manager's gradients instead recolor swatches.

Choose left/right, top/bottom, center-to-edge, edge-to-center or Edge feather. Linear/radial fades use Start/End opacity, with Swap ends and Reset fade. Opaque content bounds spans the sprite's visible bounds; disable it to span its full canvas. Limit fade depth confines the ramp to the chosen pixel distance from its start; pixels deeper than that stay untouched. Canvas selection limits writes, including arbitrary mask holes, while the gradient still spans the sprite's content/canvas. Selection edges do not become feather outlines.

Edge feather follows the full original silhouette, including transparent holes and the canvas edge. Edge opacity ramps to solid at Feather width; deeper pixels stay unchanged. It uses the classic two-pass chamfer distance. As in classic, a width of one leaves opaque pixels solid. Noise/dissolve is deterministic for the coordinates, source color, library index and Seed; reordering sprites can change a later noise preview. Checker uses the classic 2x2 thresholds and Bayer the classic 4x4 thresholds. Ordered patterns ignore Seed. Release a dragged control or press Enter after numeric input to update the reviewed output.

Trim changed sprites is optional and defaults off. It crops only sprites that actually lose pixels; unaffected included sprites are not trimmed. Primary/active secondary anchors and point coordinates are rebased to keep placement. Anchor/point overflow blocks trim until disabled or the row is excluded. Empty results keep the original canvas and anchors. A geometry change clears canvas selection through the normal document refresh. With trim off, row padding, dimensions, anchors, points, baselines, names, raw metadata and identities stay fixed. Baselines remain the originally loaded packing reference even after trim.

The per-sprite before/after base-pixel preview uses the same planned output as Apply. Trimmed borders are outlined and the crop offset/dimensions are reported. Uncheck rows to exclude them. Changed rows with an overlay (even hidden/off-canvas) or active alternate references need resolution or exclusion; unknown palette indices require repair/exclusion. No matching changes or an empty included scope add no history or discard Redo. Apply uses one Sprite opacity gradient Undo step for all included fades and trims. Cancel changes nothing. Changed raw pixels/padding/palettes/points/alternate/layer/metadata or document generation invalidate review; forged output or allocation failures cannot publish partially. Reviews are bounded to 2000 sprites, 8192 per dimension, 4 Mi-pixel sprites, 64 Mi-pixel total work and a 256 MiB pixel/scratch budget.

Changing silhouettes or geometry may affect LOAD2 packing. Verify packing before building game data. Save and Save As always retain the original IMG version and record layout. Sprite Ramp Gradient is a separate pixel-recolor workflow.)STUDIO_HELP"},
    {"Stored animation metadata",R"STUDIO_HELP(In IMG animations, select a sequence or script and an entry, then choose Edit stored metadata in the inspector. This ports the classic raw editor's record flags, Start X/Y, six damage-reference bytes and three selected-entry spare words into one review. Empty records can edit header fields but have no entry spare words. Sequence entry numbers follow playback order, the reverse of stored disk order; script calls keep forward order.

Flags use the full unsigned 16-bit range (0–65535), with a hexadecimal readout. Start coordinates and entry spare words use signed 16-bit values (-32768–32767). Start positions/flags are stored game/WIMP metadata; this review does not execute them, change Studio's stage origin, or infer game behavior. Record type comes from the IMG sequence/script lists, independently of the raw flags.

Damage selectors resolve existing global damage-table references to sequence/script names. None stores -1. Existing unusual or unresolved bytes remain unchanged unless edited; new bindings require a resolved table row that fits a signed byte and the IMG's original layout. The global damage table itself is not edited. Near layouts cannot acquire damage bindings. Names, entry order/count, targets, ticks, deltas, pointer bytes, reserved bytes and every other record stay fixed.

Review the changed-byte count, then Apply metadata for one Undo step. Cancel changes nothing; unchanged reviews add no history or discard Redo. Apply revalidates the complete stored animation blob, counts, damage table, buffer-script bytes and document identity/generation. Stale/foreign/forged reviews or insufficient memory cannot publish partial changes. Pixels, palettes, anchors, point tables, baselines, padding and sprite identities stay fixed. Save and Save As retain the original IMG version and record layout. This is metadata editing, not script runtime execution or whole-record deletion.)STUDIO_HELP"},
    {"Remap Similar Regions",R"STUDIO_HELP(Select a feature with Marquee, Lasso or Wand, choose an existing opaque destination swatch, then choose Palette > Remap Similar Regions or the selection inspector's button. In Palette manager, right-click the destination swatch for Remap Similar Regions to this color; select the artwork sprite's own palette first. Sprite marks, manager preview browsing, selected swatch sets and stored animation sequences do not restrict this operation. The review covers the source palette's primary users in the current IMG.

The exact selected opaque source pixels are sampled, excluding pixels already using the destination index. Mask holes remain outside. Other frames use the classic animation-point-relative seed and connected-component heuristic with size/distance gates. These regions can expand beyond the source mask or match the wrong feature. Inspect every included row's outline and before/after preview. Already destination-colored pixels do not seed or bridge matches; a selection containing only them or transparency needs a different source feature or destination.

Matched includes frames with suggested features by default. Source only, All, None and individual checkboxes change scope; the source can also be excluded. No-match rows start unchecked, and including one changes nothing. Destination color is restricted to existing nonzero slots. Changing it rematches every frame and resets scope to Matched, so inspect the new suggestions before Apply. The picker remains available when the chosen destination leaves no usable source sample.

Only matched pixels on included frames change to the destination index. The review counts changed indices, actual RGB changes and blocked frames. Frames with actual remaps and an overlay (including hidden/off-canvas overlays) or active alternate references need resolution or exclusion. Palette words, count/depth, index-zero transparency, silhouettes, outside pixels, excluded frames, assignments, IDs, metadata, point tables, baselines and row padding remain fixed. This directly recolors the feature; Isolate colors across frames instead preserves its current appearance for later shared palette editing.

Apply records one Remap similar regions Undo step, keeps the canvas selection and highlights the destination. Pixel, palette and swatch clipboards remain independent. Empty or unchanged scope adds no history and retains Redo. Cancel changes nothing. Apply recaptures and rematches; changed source pixels, colors, anchors, layers or membership invalidate review. Allocation failure cannot apply partially. Capture is bounded to 2000 palette users, 8192 per dimension, 4 Mi-pixel sprites, a 256 MiB pixel/mask/output/scratch budget and 64 Mi matching steps. Narrow the feature or split the library if limits are reached. Save and Save As retain the original IMG version and record layout.)STUDIO_HELP"},
    {"Isolate colors across frames",R"STUDIO_HELP(Select a feature on the artwork canvas, then choose Palette > Isolate selection across frames. The current-sprite isolation review has Across frames, and Palette manager's swatch context offers the same action on the source palette. This reviews every primary user of that palette in the current IMG, independently of sprite marks, palette swatch selection and stored animation sequences.

The source frame keeps the exact selected opaque pixels, including mask holes. Other frames use the classic heuristic: up to 768 animation-point-relative seeds seek nearby matching source indices, flood connected components and reject implausible area/centroid changes. Other-frame regions may expand beyond the source mask and can identify the wrong feature. Inspect the matched outline and before/after sprite on every included row. This is a suggestion, not game-runtime or motion analysis.

Source stays included. Matched selects source plus frames with a suggested region; Source only leaves just the pinned source; All explicitly includes no-match frames too. Orange no-match rows start unchecked. Including one moves every reserved-index pixel there away from the feature's indices, so it will no longer follow a later feature recolor. Unchecking a frame retains its pixels. Omitted/alternate-only user counts show references that can still recolor with the reserved slots.

A single shared palette plan gathers contested colors outside the regions of all included frames. Feature indices and colors stay fixed; outside pixels move to exact stored-word matches, globally unused slots, or appended duplicates. Live slots include primary/active-alternate users and hidden/off-canvas overlays. Full-palette fallback to nearest unreserved colors requires Allow approximate outside colors when RGB changes. Inclusions or packing changes reset approval. Every contested color needs a destination. Included frames with actual remaps and an overlay or active alternate references must be resolved or excluded; the source cannot be excluded.

Review matched masks, per-frame outside pixels, selected scope, omitted-user references, palette size/depth, shared destination counts and PPP impact. Apply records one Undo step for all included pixel changes and the palette. Selected reserved swatches are ready for later recoloring; no feature is recolored automatically. Already-exclusive included scopes are no-ops and retain Redo. Cancel changes nothing; stale sources, anchors, masks, membership, unknown indices or insufficient memory cannot apply partially. Apply recaptures and rematches before publication.

Capture is bounded to 2000 palette users, 8192 per dimension, 4 Mi-pixel sprites, a 256 MiB pixel/mask/output/scratch budget and 64 Mi matching steps. Narrow the feature or split the library if limits are reached. Selection, transparency, silhouettes, metadata, baseline/point data, padding, IDs, assignments and independent clipboards stay intact. Palette growth raises depth only as needed. Save keeps the original IMG version/layout; verify LOAD2 packing after count/depth growth.)STUDIO_HELP"},
    {"Isolate selection colors",R"STUDIO_HELP(Make a canvas selection with Marquee, Lasso or Wand, then choose Palette > Isolate selection colors or the selection inspector's button. Palette manager's color context menu also has Isolate canvas selection colors; select the artwork sprite's own palette first. The target is captured from the current artwork sprite and canvas mask, independently of manager preview browsing or selected swatches.

Selected opaque base pixels keep their original indices. Colors also used outside the selection are contested; those outside pixels move to other slots. Mask holes count as outside. The selected region can then be recolored independently of the rest of this sprite. Transparent pixels stay zero. This operation does not recolor the feature itself and is different from Variant paint, which changes selected colors while preserving other palettes.

Destinations use a full-word exact non-reserved color first, then slots unused by every primary/active-alternate palette user and all overlays (including hidden/off-canvas), then appended duplicates. Stored high bits are copied. If all 256 slots are in use, nearest unreserved colors can approximate the outside artwork; Allow approximate outside colors is required when RGB changes. Changing packing settings resets approval. If any contested color has no destination, the whole operation is blocked. A target with actual outside remaps plus an overlay or active alternate references is blocked until resolved. Other users and their indices stay unchanged.

Review reserved colors, outside mappings, recycled/appended/approximate destinations, color count/depth, changed pixels, packing PPP impact and before/after composite previews. Show selection outline identifies the protected mask. Other-user previews and counts identify frames still sharing the reserved indices. This is current-sprite isolation only: other frames can still recolor with the feature. Use Across frames to open the separate matching/inclusion review; the current-sprite operation does not propagate.

Isolate colors commits palette and outside pixels in one Undo step. Reserved swatches are selected in Palette manager for subsequent recoloring. The canvas mask remains intact. Already-exclusive selections change nothing and retain Undo/Redo history; missing or transparent-only selections are refused. Cancel changes nothing. Changed data invalidates review; allocation failures preserve data/history/Redo. Palette growth raises declared depth only when required; index zero, silhouettes, baselines, point tables, metadata, padding, sprite IDs and assignments remain unchanged. All clipboards remain independent. Save retains the existing IMG version and layout. Verify LOAD2 packing after growth/depth changes.)STUDIO_HELP"},
    {"Move palette colors to end",R"STUDIO_HELP(In Palette > Manage palettes, select opaque swatches with click, Ctrl-toggle or Shift-range, then choose Move to end. The swatch context menu has Move selected colors to end; when nothing is selected it uses that clicked opaque color. The review lets you change the selected set, choose All opaque, or clear it with None. Index zero stays protected.

All unselected opaque colors come first, then all selected colors. Each group keeps its original order, including nonadjacent selections and unused colors. Complete stored color words travel with them. The result grid and old-to-new index list show the new order. Count, declared depth, metadata and index zero remain fixed. This differs from Reverse selected order, which recolors fixed pixel indices.

All primary palette users remap together to keep their appearance. Sprite marks and canvas pixel selections do not limit this shared edit. Review every primary and active alternate user. Overlays and alternate appearances whose referenced indices would move are blocked; flatten/remove the overlay or resolve alternate references first. Reordering unused colors is allowed when every guarded base/layer index stays fixed. Alternate-only previews show the affected appearance without pretending to remap their other primary palettes. No user can be excluded.

Move colors to end commits in one Undo step; Cancel changes nothing. The chosen swatches and highlighted color follow their new indices, while the canvas selection stays intact. Already-final and all-opaque selections are no-ops with no new history and retain Redo. Empty selections are refused. Changed sources invalidate review; allocation failure leaves artwork/history/Redo intact. Pixel, palette and swatch clipboards stay independent. Baselines, points, pixel padding, identities and assignments stay unchanged. Save retains the existing IMG version and layout.)STUDIO_HELP"},
    {"Copy zero color to opaque",R"STUDIO_HELP(Palette > Manage palettes > #0 to opaque copies index zero's complete stored RGB555 word into a nonzero slot. Right-click a swatch for automatic placement or Copy #0 here. Index zero itself stays unchanged and transparent. Copy color only / leave pixels is the default; the copied slot is ready for painting without changing any sprite indices.

Automatic placement appends while fewer than 256 colors exist. With a full palette it first finds a slot unused by all primary and active alternate users and all overlays, including hidden/off-canvas overlays; otherwise it reuses a word identical to index zero. If neither exists, choose an explicit destination. Growing to an explicit slot fills intervening gaps with black. Growth raises declared depth only when needed; it never lowers depth.

Remap current artwork sprite replaces every base zero pixel with the destination, including transparent canvas background. Remap canvas selection only uses the canvas mask captured when opening the review and retains holes. The target is the selected artwork sprite on this palette; changing the manager's preview sprite does not select a different target. A target with an overlay or active alternate references is blocked when remapping would make pixels opaque. Copy-only remains available. Other sprites' pixel indices and assignments stay fixed.

Review destination-before/after colors, growth, declared depth, shared primary/alternate users, existing destination usage and before/after composite previews. A used destination with different RGB requires Allow recoloring existing destination users; this affects all shared users, including overlays. Changing settings resets approval. Appending avoids that recoloring. A changed library invalidates the review.

Copy zero color commits the color and optional remapping in one Undo step; Cancel changes nothing. A copy that is already identical and remaps no pixels adds no Undo entry and keeps Redo. Allocation failure keeps colors, pixels and history intact. Pixel, whole-palette and swatch clipboards stay independent. Save keeps the original IMG version and layout. Palette growth may cross LOAD2 PPP limits, and making pixels opaque changes silhouette packing; verify LOAD2 packing before building game data.)STUDIO_HELP"},
    {"Palettes & color wheel",R"STUDIO_HELP(The left color wheel edits the selected palette entry. These colors are shared by every sprite using that palette. Index zero is transparent and locked. RGB555 stores 0-31 per channel; UI RGB/HSV/hex controls are quantized to those values. A wheel drag is one Undo step; Escape cancels it.

Palette > Manage palettes has separate staged color edits and a sprite preview. Browsing does not assign palettes. Apply colors changes all users in one Undo step. Reset or close discards unapplied colors. Choose Make palette private to isolate one sprite before recoloring.

Select swatches with click, Ctrl-toggle or Shift-range; All opaque selects every nonzero slot, while None leaves no colors selected. Invert selected previews the RGB555 complement, preserving stored high bits. Reverse selected order reverses complete color words across the selected slot numbers, including nonadjacent slots; it needs at least two selected colors. Both keep index zero locked and leave pixel indices, palette size/depth and assignments unchanged. They can build on current RGB/HSL previews or each other. Repeating either effect twice cancels that effect. Review the shared sprite appearance, then Apply colors for one Undo step; Reset or Close discards the preview. With no selected opaque slots, the effect buttons are disabled.

Ctrl+C copies every marked palette in library order, or the selected palette when nothing is marked. The search filter does not exclude marked palettes. Right-click Copy and choose Copy selected only to ignore marks. Ctrl+V / Paste appends the entire captured set in one Undo step, selects its first new palette, and leaves sprite and alternate assignments unchanged. Existing names are retained when unique; collisions receive numbered names. Colors (including stored high bits), declared depth and palette metadata travel with the capture; raw name tails stay intact unless a collision renames the palette. Clipboard contents survive source edits and closure until replaced, cleared or Studio closes. Failed capture keeps the previous clipboard; failed paste adds nothing. Clear beside Paste releases it without editing the IMG. Pixel, sprite and animation clipboards stay separate. Save separately; the IMG version and layout never change. These keys target palettes while the manager is open. Up/Down browses, Shift+R focuses Rename and Enter applies. Delete removes unused palettes only. Undo/Redo uses this IMG's palette history. Pending color, merge, ramp or usage previews block unrelated palette mutations.

Right-click a swatch or use Copy / paste colors to copy that color or the Ctrl/Shift-selected slots. This separate color clipboard owns RGB555 words and original indices; source edits/closure do not affect it. Original-index paste recolors shared users and can grow the palette with black gaps. Append retains existing colors; every copied color must fit within 256 slots or the whole paste is refused. One copied color can also replace an existing opaque slot. Destination index zero stays unchanged; copy its color to an opaque slot or append it instead. Review the before/after sprite, source-to-target indices, changed slots, shared primary/alternate references, palette growth, depth and LOAD2 PPP impact. Apply records one Undo step without remapping pixels, assignments or metadata; Cancel leaves artwork and clipboard unchanged. Clear copied colors releases only this clipboard. Pending previews block clipboard mutations; a changed library invalidates its review. Save separately in the original IMG format. Ctrl+C/V still targets whole palettes.

Clean copy in Palette manager creates a new unassigned palette from the source's used colors. The review shows its uniquely numbered name, source/copy color counts, optional depth and sorted result. Dark to light is the default; original order and hue families are available. Usage includes base pixels, active alternate references and all opaque overlay colors, including hidden/off-canvas overlays. Base colors covered by an overlay stay retained. An unused palette retains every color. Copy sorting is allowed even with alternate users/overlays because source pixels, colors, layers and assignments stay unchanged. Create clean copy appends once with one Undo step; it does not assign sprites. Source metadata/high bits are retained; naming follows ordinary palette-copy collision rules. Cancel adds nothing. Stale/changed sources, capacity or allocation failure cannot append partially. Save separately in the existing IMG version/layout. All clipboards remain independent.

Palette > Alternate costume > Borrow material colors builds a copy in this palette's existing slot layout using colors from a different reference palette. Name each material and enter ranges on both sides (for example Cloth: 1-5, 10-32 here; 31-48 in reference). Add up to 16 sections; enable only those to borrow. Enabled sections cannot overlap on either palette. The grids highlight selected slots; source/copy sprite previews show the result. Saved mappings opens one-palette layouts and paired templates for reuse.

Shade matching offers Equal shade weights, This palette's pixel coverage, or Costume family's pixel coverage (default). Coverage sorts shades by brightness and interpolates reference shades by their share of base pixels. It excludes padding, overlays and alternate-only users. Family pools same-size palettes sharing at least a quarter of opaque slots, independent of section ranges or order. Counts show pooled palettes/sprites and per-section pixels. A section with no coverage on one side uses equal shade weights on that side. Reference colors, family colors and captured sprite data are rechecked at Apply; stale reviews must be reopened. Index zero, unselected source colors and stored high bits stay fixed. The copy keeps source color count/depth/metadata. Automatic borrowing names combine source/reference stems. Create-only or optional selected/marked/all primary assignments undo with the copy. Alternate references and original palettes/pixels stay unchanged. Save keeps the IMG's existing version and record layout.

Saved costume mappings: in Borrow material colors, click Saved mappings. Paired templates store both sides; one-palette layouts replace only This palette or Reference palette, pairing material names without regard to case. Layouts associated with the selected palette name/count are suggested for review. No mapping is applied automatically. Mismatched color counts, out-of-bounds ranges and overlapping sections are blocked without clipping. Unmatched names stay visible as disabled materials; fill the missing side and enable them before creating a copy. Use mapping updates the costume draft; it does not edit IMG data or add Undo history. Continue reviewing before Create costume palette.

Save current stores enabled materials only. Preset names allow 1-31 characters and saved material names 1-15 characters without separators | ; = , (rename longer material names before saving). Colors, tint strength, coverage choice and sprite assignments are not saved. Replace existing must be checked to update a matching name. Delete selected preset removes only that saved entry; current draft sections stay intact. Saving/deleting is immediate and persists even if you later Cancel the costume review. Reload presets rereads external edits; failed/malformed reads block writes, and changed files require Reload before replacement. Each file holds at most 256 presets / 1 MiB. Studio uses palette_layouts.txt and costume_templates.txt in its settings folder (hover the filename for its path), compatible with the original tool's text format. The classic tool's settings are not read or changed automatically. Presets are never written into IMG files; Save and Save As retain the existing IMG version and record layout.

Palette > Alternate costume > Tint material ramps creates a new palette with chosen ramps recolored using the classic ColorizeRamp luminance-preserving tint. The first detected ramp starts selected for preview; confirm that it is the intended material. Pick a tint or preset and strength, select more ramps, or adjust the ramp split angle (which resets picked ramps). Manual slot ranges such as 1-5, 10-32 can isolate trim/skin inside a detected ramp; they clip to opaque slots and support nonadjacent ranges. Index zero and unselected colors stay unchanged; stored high bits are retained. The new palette keeps the source's color count, declared depth and other metadata; its new name clears old raw-name bytes. Blank name uses the source stem and tint hue; existing names get a unique number shown in review. Names accept up to 9 letters, digits or underscores.

Create only / unassigned is the default. Optionally assign the new palette to the selected sprite, marked sprites on the source palette, or all primary source users. Whole sprites are assigned independently of canvas selection. Pixel indices, baselines, point tables, overlays and alternate references remain unchanged; primary alternate users may use the costume, while alternate-only users stay on their existing primary palette. Before/after previews include visible overlays; hidden/off-canvas overlays stay intact. An unreferenced source can create an unassigned copy using palette previews. Tint strength zero creates an exact copy. Create adds the palette and assignments in one Undo step; Cancel changes nothing. Changed source data invalidates review, and allocation/capacity failure leaves data/history/Redo intact. Pixel and palette clipboards are independent. Save keeps the original IMG version/record layout. Saved mappings keeps reusable sections in Studio settings, independently of the IMG.

Palette > Transfer palette by ramps moves the selected sprite's source palette users onto a different palette. Choose Selected sprite, Marked on source palette, or All on source palette; the canvas pixel selection does not limit this operation. Other source palettes stay outside the scope. The destination starts with a marked palette when available. Automatic ramp detection and hue-based pairing provide suggestions: confirm each source range and its destination/result swatches, adjust source/target split angles, choose another target ramp or explicit nearest-color fallback, or Reset ramp suggestions. Relative shading maps each source ramp's dark/light positions across the destination ramp; Match brightness chooses nearest luminance inside the paired ramp. Unpaired/uncovered slots use nearest opaque color across the destination; counts identify that fallback. Scope pixel counts include excluded sprites. Select rows to preview before/after base pixels; uncheck rows to exclude them. Included overlays and remaps that would alter active alternate appearances require resolution or exclusion. Identity maps retain alternate tables. Allow color changes is required when included pixels change appearance; changing settings or inclusions resets approval. The review shows PPP color-limit changes. Apply transfers included sprites in one Undo step; Cancel changes nothing. Both palettes, their stored words/depth/metadata, sprite identities, baselines, point tables, pixel padding and independent clipboards remain unchanged. Opaque pixels never map to zero; transparency stays zero. Changed palette/source data invalidates review; allocation failure preserves document/history. Save keeps the IMG version and record layout. Check LOAD2 packing before building game data.

Downscale in Palette manager reviews a palette with a 4-8 bpp limit (16/32/64/128/256 total colors, including transparency). If the original count already fits, it retains all colors and indices and changes only declared depth. Otherwise it packs active opaque slots exactly when they fit, or uses the classic pixel-weighted median cut with up to eight refinements. Final matching uses the actual stored RGB555 colors. Before/after previews, result swatches and counts show approximated colors, changed pixels, remapped indices, depth and PPP fallback. Allow approximate colors is required for color loss, including an unreferenced palette; changing the limit resets approval. Index zero remains transparent and opaque pixels never map to zero. An unreferenced palette gives every opaque slot equal weight; a transparency-only palette may pack to one entry. Names, other metadata, assignments, baselines, point tables and pixel padding stay fixed. Exact packing retains complete stored words; weighted merging generates RGB555 representatives. Alternate references and overlays block downscaling until resolved. Apply is one Undo step; Cancel leaves the library unchanged. Changed sources invalidate review; allocation failure leaves colors, pixels and history intact. Save retains the existing IMG version/layout. Verify LOAD2 packing after color-count or declared-depth changes.

Inherit colors from marked: mark exactly one other palette in the library, select the destination and choose Inherit colors from marked. The destination keeps its name, metadata and sprite assignments; its colors, color count, declared depth and padding follow the marked source. Every destination sprite matches its opaque pixels to the nearest opaque source slot. RGB distance matches the classic tool by default; perceptual matching is optional. The review shows before/after sprites, approximation counts and packing impact. Allow approximate colors is required when used colors change; changing matching resets that approval. Transparency stays at index 0. Active alternate references and overlays block inheritance until resolved to protect shared appearances. Source colors and sprites remain unchanged. Apply is one Undo step; Cancel changes nothing. Changed sources invalidate the review. Save retains the existing IMG version and record layout; verify LOAD2 packing before building.


Mark palette rows with their checkboxes or Space; Shift+M marks all, M clears marks, and Mark shown respects the search filter. Select a destination, then * / keypad multiply or Merge marked reviews enabled sources together. Destination marks are ignored. Excluding a source leaves its mark intact. With no marks, Merge still reviews one source and destination. All sources use the final combined destination, with explicit approximation approval, one Undo step and per-source skipped/kept/removed summaries. Remove sources that become unused never removes an alternate-referenced palette. Unused marked sources stay unchanged. Marks are local view state and clear when a structural edit or history restore changes palette identities. Merge reports palette growth, changed indices, approximations and LOAD2 PPP impact. Usage / compact reviews unused colors, reference counts and resulting indices. Pack used colors together removes gaps; choose Original order, Dark to light, or Group like colors. Grouping puts neutral colors first and makes each hue family a dark-to-light ramp, using classic ordering. Before/after sprite previews stay identical because primary pixel indices remap along with whole stored color words; resulting swatches show their new order. Index zero and opaque black remain distinct; duplicate live colors remain separate. Turn packing off for tail-only trimming with fixed indices. Alternate palette users lock shared indices, so only an unused tail can be removed and sorting is disabled. Flatten/remove relevant overlays before organization. Recompute declared color depth is separate and defaults off. Review color-count, depth and PPP changes. Apply organization/compaction commits one Undo step; Cancel changes nothing. Source changes invalidate the review; raw color/metadata/pixel/alternate changes also refuse Apply. Allocation failures leave pixels, colors and history intact. Save separately in the unchanged IMG version/layout. Shading / gradients stages palette ramps. Approximate matches require review. Palette assignment offers Match colors or Keep indices, with alternate-palette constraints made explicit.)STUDIO_HELP"},
    {"Video capture & digitize",R"STUDIO_HELP(File > Capture video frames opens a frame grabber before digitize review. Scrub with Left/Right (Shift = ten), Home/End for first/last, I/O for In/Out, P to pick individual frames, and Space to play/pause. Keys pause during text entry and capture/probe jobs.

Choose an In/Out range with Every Nth frame or manual Picks. Drag a crop around the actor; right-click places a shared floor point. Wheel zooms, middle-drag pans, and Fit resets framing. Capture and digitize prepares frames without editing the IMG. The digitize review keys the background, seeds materials and fits palette ramps; import the reviewed batch to commit one Undo step.

The decoder runs an ffmpeg executable, found via IMGTOOL_FFMPEG, beside Studio, on PATH or common install folders. It is not linked as an FFmpeg library. Variable-rate source timing may need normalization for exact frame seeking. Cancel stops pending processing at its next safe boundary. Source video is unchanged.)STUDIO_HELP"},
    {"MK2 strike tables",R"STUDIO_HELP(Tools > MK2 strike tables opens a source-backed editor for MKSTK.ASM. Choose a character and move, then inspect its strike fields and preview box. Coordinates, size, routine token, hit/block damage, score and sound belong to the ASM source, not the IMG point table.

Changes are staged and reviewed before source save. Source Undo/Redo and Ctrl+S target this source document while its editor is open. Source histories and save destinations are independent of the IMG. Source reload/close/quit protect applied unsaved changes and saves reject external changes.

Strike associations binds source moves to artwork references for inspection. The strike phase inspector previews phase selections under explicit countdown/flag assumptions. These previews do not simulate startup, hit interruption, attacker/victim timing or game routines. IMG artwork and its binary format are unchanged by source inspection.)STUDIO_HELP"},
    {"Collect animation artwork",R"STUDIO_HELP(In ASM / Reactions, Review artwork inventories every expanded piece symbol. Choose explicitly among ambiguous open-library candidates. Collect artwork freezes resolved sprites and palettes into an owned review that survives source edits or closure. Partial previews require explicit consent.

Create library makes a separate unsaved IMG tab. Copy to existing IMG uses a destination review with prefixes and exclusions and one destination Undo step. It requires the same IMG version/layout; neither library is converted.

Reuse identical named sprites is opt-in. A unique existing name must match pixels, effective stored name bytes, palette contents/metadata, anchors, points, alternates and saved sprite metadata. Exact matches are reused, missing sprites appended, differing or ambiguous matches blocked. If everything is reused, no IMG data or Undo history changes. Prefixes affect copied names, not external ASM operands. No source file is saved automatically.)STUDIO_HELP"},
    {"About",R"STUDIO_HELP(Imgtool Studio is the separate modern editor for Midway arcade IMG artwork. Original imgtool began as Shawn Liptak's Williams Electronics DOS tool, now ported to C/C++, SDL2 and Dear ImGui.

IMG artwork, embedded animations, Studio projects and external ASM sources have distinct save destinations. Inspect previews and export reviews before writing game assets. Studio does not assemble game code or emulate the game runtime.)STUDIO_HELP"},
};
